TI  - Capacity control in ground source heat pump systems part II: Comparative analysis between on/off controlled and variable capacity systems
T2  - International Journal of Refrigeration
VL  - 34
PY  - 2011
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2011
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.ijrefrig.2011.05.012
AB  - In the present paper, as the second part of two, modeling and simulation was carried out for a Ground Source Heat Pump (GSHP) system in the presence of all the most important interacting sub-systems such as building, ground heat source, electrical auxiliary heater, and the heat pump unit in order to make a fair and comprehensive comparison between the annual performance of on/off controlled and variable capacity systems. The annual modeling showed that dimensioning of the on/off controlled GSHP based on the peak heat demand of the building plays a significant role when the two control strategies are compared: if the on/off controlled GSHP is dimensioned to cover only 55% of the peak heat demand of the building, the electrical auxiliary, which then covers about 10% of the annual heating demand of the building, makes the SPF of the on/off controlled GSHP to be lower than the one of the variable speed system. On the contrary, when the on/off controlled system is dimensioned to cover more than 65% of the building’s peak heat demand, i.e. more than 95% of the annual heat demand of the building, there is no considerable difference between the SPFs of the on/off controlled and variable capacity systems.
KW  - Heat pump
KW  - Ground Source Heat Pumps
KW  - Control
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. Brine to water heat pump systems like Ground Source Heat Pumps (GSHPs) are one of the common and fast growing systems for heating the buildings in USA and European countries. 
N1  - C2. The presents paper aims at making a fair comparison between the annual performance of an on/off and variable capacity GSHP systems, using the generic model developed by Madani et al (2011). 
N1  - C3. The current paper compares the annual performance of the on/off controlled and inverter-driven variable capacity heat pump systems, using the generic model developed by Madani et al (2011). 
N1  - C4. The results from the annual modeling for both systems indicate: C When the annual performance of on/off controlled GSHP system is compared with the one of the variable speed GSHP system, it must be noticed that dimensioning of the on/off controlled GSHP based on the peak heat demand of the building plays a significant role: if the on/off controlled GSHP is dimensioned to cover only 55% of the peak heat demand of the building, the important role of the electrical auxiliary heater makes the Seasonal Performance Factor (SPF) of the on/off controlled GSHP be lower than the one of the variable speed system. 
N1  - C5. On the contrary, when the on/off controlled system is dimensioned to cover more than 65% of the building’s peak heat demand, i.e. more than 95% of the annual heat demand of the building, there is no considerable difference between the SPFs of the on/off controlled and variable capacity systems. 
N1  - C6. C The energy use by liquid pumps in the variable speed GSHP system can be 5e30% higher compared to the single speed system. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. As of 2005, there are over a million units installed worldwide providing about 15 GW of thermal capacity (Rybach, 2005) and the annual global increase has been more than 10% over the last ten years (Lund et al, 2004). 
N1  - D2. As may be seen from Fig. 9, the variable capacity system has higher efficiency than unit A due to avoiding the electrical auxiliary heater; Furthermore, the modeling results show that there is no significant difference between the COP of system B, system C, and the variable speed system. 
N1  - D3. As shown in Fig. 12, the SPF of unit A is about 13% lower than the SPF of the variable speed system whereas the SPF of system B, C, and D are almost the same as the one of variable speed system. 
N1  - D4. On the contrary, when the on/off controlled system is dimensioned to cover more than 65% of the building’s peak heat demand, i.e. more than 95% of the annual heat demand of the building, there is no considerable difference between the SPFs of the on/off controlled and variable capacity systems. 
N1  - D5. C The energy use by liquid pumps in the variable speed GSHP system can be 5e30% higher compared to the single speed system. 
N1  - D6. As a final remark, in order to obtain a higher efficiency for on/off controlled systems, it is important to dimension them in a way that electrical auxiliary heater covers less than 5% of annual heat demand. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The study compares the annual performance of on/off controlled and variable capacity Ground Source Heat Pump (GSHP) systems, finding that the dimensioning of the on/off controlled GSHP plays a significant role in the comparison, with variable capacity systems performing better when the on/off controlled GSHP is dimensioned to cover only 55% of the peak heat demand.. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The study finds that the dimensioning of the on/off controlled GSHP plays a significant role in the comparison, with variable capacity systems performing better when the on/off controlled GSHP is dimensioned to cover only 55% of the peak heat demand. Additionally, the study finds that when the on/off controlled system is dimensioned to cover more than 65% of the building’s peak heat demand, there is no considerable difference between the SPFs of the on/off controlled and variable capacity systems.. 
N1  - E5. The study finds that the variable speed GSHP unit has a higher balance point temperature than the single speed units, resulting in a smaller compressor cycling region. The variable speed unit also shows higher efficiency in certain temperature ranges, but higher energy consumption due to the lack of need for an electrical auxiliary heater.. 
N1  - E6. The key findings of the study are that the dimensioning of on/off controlled heat pump systems plays a significant role in their performance, and that the use of electrical auxiliary heaters can significantly impact energy consumption. Additionally, the study found that the energy use by liquid pumps in the variable speed GSHP system can be 5-30% higher compared to the single speed system.. 
N1  - E7. On the contrary, when the on/off controlled system is dimensioned to cover more than 65% of the building’s peak heat demand, i.e. more than 95% of the annual heat demand of the building, there is no considerable difference between the SPFs of the on/off controlled and variable capacity systems. 
N1  - E8. C The energy use by liquid pumps in the variable speed GSHP system can be 5e30% higher compared to the single speed system. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The objectives of the study are to compare the annual performance of on/off controlled and inverter-driven variable capacity heat pump systems, and to investigate the impact of dimensioning and electrical auxiliary heaters on energy consumption.. 
N1  - E11. Methods. 
N1  - E12. The study uses modeling and simulation to evaluate the annual performance of the two control strategies, with all the systems modeled over a year. The study also uses a quasi-static model to consider the dynamic interactions among the system components.. 
N1  - E13. The study uses a modeling approach to estimate the building's heat demand and the performance of the GSHP units. The model takes into account various parameters such as solar insolation, thermal inertia, and user behavior. The study also uses data from the Meteonorm database to simulate the climatic conditions in Stockholm.. 
N1  - E14. The methods used in the study include modeling and simulation of the heat pump systems, as well as validation with experimental data from in-situ field measurements.. 
N1  - E15. Results. 
N1  - E16. The study finds that the dimensioning of the on/off controlled GSHP plays a significant role in the comparison, with variable capacity systems performing better when the on/off controlled GSHP is dimensioned to cover only 55% of the peak heat demand. The study also finds that when the on/off controlled system is dimensioned to cover more than 65% of the building’s peak heat demand, there is no considerable difference between the SPFs of the on/off controlled and variable capacity systems.. 
N1  - E17. The study finds that the variable speed GSHP unit has a higher balance point temperature than the single speed units, resulting in a smaller compressor cycling region. The variable speed unit also shows higher efficiency in certain temperature ranges, but higher energy consumption due to the lack of need for an electrical auxiliary heater. The study also finds that the unit A and unit B have higher total energy consumption than the variable speed system over a year due to high annual energy consumption of the electrical auxiliary heater.. 
N1  - E18. The results of the study show that the SPF of the on/off controlled systems can be lower than that of the variable speed system, especially when the on/off controlled system is dimensioned to cover only a small percentage of the peak heat demand. The results also show that the energy use by liquid pumps in the variable speed GSHP system can be 5-30% higher compared to the single speed system.. 
N1  - E19. Conclusions. 
N1  - E20. The study concludes that the dimensioning of the on/off controlled GSHP plays a significant role in the comparison, with variable capacity systems performing better when the on/off controlled GSHP is dimensioned to cover only 55% of the peak heat demand. The study also concludes that when the on/off controlled system is dimensioned to cover more than 65% of the building’s peak heat demand, there is no considerable difference between the SPFs of the on/off controlled and variable capacity systems.. 
N1  - E21. The study concludes that the variable speed GSHP unit has higher efficiency in certain temperature ranges, but also higher energy consumption due to the lack of need for an electrical auxiliary heater. The study also concludes that the unit A and unit B have higher total energy consumption than the variable speed system over a year due to high annual energy consumption of the electrical auxiliary heater.. 
N1  - E22. The conclusions of the study are that the dimensioning of on/off controlled heat pump systems is crucial for their performance, and that the use of electrical auxiliary heaters can significantly impact energy consumption. The study also concludes that retrofitting an inverter to existing single speed HP systems can increase their annual efficiency. 
