TI  - An optimization strategy for the control of small capacity heat pump integrated air-conditioning system
T2  - Energy Conversion and Management
VL  - 119
PY  - 2016
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2016
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.enconman.2016.04.027
AB  - This paper studies the optimization of a small-scale central air-conditioning system, in which the cooling is provided by a ground source heat pump (GSHP) equipped with an on/off capacity control. The optimization strategy aims to optimize the overall system energy consumption and simultaneously guarantee the robustness of the space air temperature control without violating the allowed GSHP maximum start-ups number per hour specified by customers. The set-point of the chilled water return temperature and the width of the water temperature control band are used as the decision variables for the optimization. The performance of the proposed strategy was tested on a simulation platform. Results show that the optimization strategy can save the energy consumption by 9.59% in a typical spring day and 2.97% in a typical summer day. Meanwhile it is able to enhance the space air temperature control robustness when compared with a basic control strategy without optimization.
KW  - ground source heat pump
KW  - Optimization
KW  - control strategy
KW  - Air-conditioning system
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. This paper studies the optimization of a small-scale A/C system integrated with a ground source heat pump (GSHP). 
N1  - C2. This paper presents an optimization strategy for a GSHPintegrated A/C system in order to optimize the overall system energy consumption without violating the allowed GSHP maximum start-ups number and degrading the space air temperature control performance. 
N1  - C3. The performance of the optimization strategy is compared with the basic control strategy. 
N1  - C4. The results show that the optimization strategy can improve the robustness of the space air temperature control significantly and reduce the system energy consumption obviously in spring days, i.e. under light load conditions. 
N1  - C5. The energy consumption of the GSHP was reduced by 21.15% and the overall system was reduced by 9.59%. 
N1  - C6. Both the optimization strategy and the base control strategy can achieve a robust control of the space air temperature, while the energy consumption of the overall system is slightly smaller when the optimization strategy is used. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. It has been shown that more than 40% of the building energy consumptions are mainly used for the heating, ventilation, and air-conditioning (HVAC) systems [1,2]. 
N1  - D2. The energy consumption of the GSHP was reduced by 21.15% and the overall system was reduced by 9.59%. 
N1  - D3. In the summer day, both the optimization strategy and the base control strategy can achieve a robust control of the space air temperature, while the energy consumption of the overall system is slightly smaller (reduced by 2.97%) when the optimization strategy is used. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The study proposes an optimization scheme to improve the energy performance of a ground source heat pump (GSHP) integrated air conditioning (A/C) system by optimizing the chilled water return (CHWR) temperature set-point and the temperature control band.. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The proposed optimization strategy can save energy consumption by 9.59% in a typical spring day and 2.97% in a typical summer day, while enhancing the space air temperature control robustness. The strategy optimizes the set-point of the chilled water return temperature and the width of the water temperature control band to minimize overall system energy consumption.. 
N1  - E5. The study finds that optimizing the CHWR temperature set-point and the temperature control band can improve the energy performance of the GSHP integrated A/C system, reduce the on/off cycling, and enhance the room temperature control performance.. 
N1  - E6. The study finds that the proposed optimization algorithm can achieve good control robustness and minimize energy consumption, with an average absolute tracking error of 0.030 °C. The GSHP model and GSHE model are validated using experimental data, with a maximum mean deviation of 2.46% for compressor power consumption and 4.45% for chilled water temperature.. 
N1  - E7. The optimization strategy can reduce the energy consumption of the GSHP by 21.15% and the overall system by 9.59% in spring days, and by 6.34% and 2.97% in summer days, respectively. The optimization strategy also improves the robustness of space air temperature control.. 
N1  - E8. Both the optimization strategy and the base control strategy can achieve a robust control of the space air temperature, while the energy consumption of the overall system is slightly smaller when the optimization strategy is used. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The objective of the study is to propose an optimization strategy for a GSHP-integrated A/C system to minimize overall system energy consumption subject to the allowed GSHP maximum start-ups number per hour specified by customers.. 
N1  - E11. Methods. 
N1  - E12. The study uses a simulation platform to test the performance of the proposed strategy. The optimization strategy involves two steps: checking the water volume inside the chilled water loop and optimizing the set-point of the chilled water return temperature and the width of the water temperature control band.. 
N1  - E13. The study uses a simulation-based approach to test the performance of the optimization strategy, with a simulated virtual system representing an actual GSHP-integrated central A/C system.. 
N1  - E14. The study uses a simulation platform constructed using TRNSYS software, which includes models for the building, GSHP, AHU, water pump, water tank, GSHE, and temperature and humidity sensors. The optimization algorithm is coded using MATLAB and embedded in TRNSYS. The study uses experimental data to validate the GSHP model and GSHE model.. 
N1  - E15. The optimization strategy involves two steps: checking the water volume inside the chilled water loop and developing an optimization algorithm to optimize the system settings (DTw and Tw,set) by minimizing the total power of the system. The performance of the optimization strategy is compared with the basic control strategy using simulation data.. 
N1  - E16. Results. 
N1  - E17. The results show that the proposed optimization strategy can save energy consumption and enhance the space air temperature control robustness. The strategy also optimizes the set-point of the chilled water return temperature and the width of the water temperature control band to minimize overall system energy consumption.. 
N1  - E18. The study finds that the proposed optimization algorithm can achieve good control robustness and minimize energy consumption. The GSHP model and GSHE model are validated using experimental data, with a maximum mean deviation of 2.46% for compressor power consumption and 4.45% for chilled water temperature.. 
N1  - E19. The results show that the optimization strategy can reduce the energy consumption of the GSHP and the overall system, and improve the robustness of space air temperature control. The optimization strategy can also guarantee the GSHP start-ups number specified by customers.. 
N1  - E20. Conclusions. 
N1  - E21. The study concludes that the proposed optimization strategy is effective in minimizing overall system energy consumption while guaranteeing the robustness of space air temperature control. The strategy can be used to optimize the performance of GSHP-integrated A/C systems.. 
N1  - E22. The study concludes that optimizing the CHWR temperature set-point and the temperature control band is essential to improve the energy performance of the GSHP integrated A/C system.. 
N1  - E23. The optimization strategy can efficiently reduce the energy consumption of the overall system, especially under partial load conditions, and improve the robustness of space air temperature control. The proposed strategy is not difficult to use and can be implemented in existing GSHP integrated A/C systems. 
