N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The study aimed to minimize the operating cost of a system using an epsilon-constrained differential evolution algorithm with random jumping (eDE-RJ) to optimize the operation of ground source heat pumps (GSHPs) and other machines, considering constraints such as upper and lower bounds of circulating fluid temperature and maximum thermal output.. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The proposed method, eDE-RJ, can solve nearly all possible configurations and is suitable for nonlinear configurations. The optimal operations achieved by the proposed method can reduce operating costs by at least 3.78% and at most 12.56% compared to empirical operations.. 
N1  - E5. The key findings of the study include the development of a model to calculate the borefield temperature and the GSHP's inlet temperature, and the evaluation of the impact of dynamic electricity prices on the system's operation.. 
N1  - E6. The simplified optimization strategy that determined only the start and stop times of the GSHP operation could reduce the operating costs compared to the empirical operation for a single day and 7 days by 3.78% and 9.59%, respectively. 
N1  - E7. The detailed optimization strategy that determined the load rates of all the components in each time step could reduce the operating costs compared to the empirical operation for a single day and 7 days by 6.81% and 12.56%, respectively. 
N1  - E8. The proposed method can be widely used in both theoretical and practical studies when users want to obtain an optimal operation schedule. It remains to address how this method can be applied to a much longer period such as for an analysis of the life-cycle impact of GSHP, thermal interaction among BHEs, and optimal GSHP design. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The objective of the study is to determine the optimal operations of a hybrid GSHP system, considering dynamic ground temperature variation and machine performance. Another objective is to reduce operating costs and energy consumption.. 
N1  - E11. Methods. 
N1  - E12. The proposed optimization method, eDE-RJ, is based on the differential evolution (DE) algorithm, categorized as the metaheuristics of an artificial intelligence method. The method is used to determine the optimal operation of a hybrid GSHP system, considering dynamic ground temperature variation and machine performance.. 
N1  - E13. The methods used in the study include the development of a model to calculate the borefield temperature and the GSHP's inlet temperature, using the infinite line source (ILS) model and temporal and spatial superposition techniques.. 
N1  - E14. The method used in the study is an epsilon-constrained differential evolution algorithm with random jumping (eDE-RJ), which is a metaheuristic optimization method.. 
N1  - E15. The study uses the proposed eDE-RJ method, which can efficiently solve highly nonlinear configurations and iterative calculations to calculate the ground temperature, and compares the results with empirical operation.. 
N1  - E16. Results. 
N1  - E17. The results show that the proposed method, eDE-RJ, can reduce operating costs by at least 3.78% and at most 12.56% compared to empirical operations. The method can also derive the solution rapidly while maintaining high computation accuracy.. 
N1  - E18. The results of the study include the calculation of the borefield temperature and the GSHP's inlet temperature, and the evaluation of the impact of dynamic electricity prices on the system's operation.. 
N1  - E19. The results of the study show that the optimal operation schedules for GSHPs and other machines result in reduced operating costs, and that considering thermal history and partial load operation is important in the optimization.. 
N1  - E20. Conclusions. 
N1  - E21. The study concludes that the proposed method, eDE-RJ, is effective in determining the optimal operations of a hybrid GSHP system, considering dynamic ground temperature variation and machine performance. The method can reduce operating costs and energy consumption, making it a useful tool for practical applications.. 
N1  - E22. The conclusions of the study are that the developed model can be used to calculate the borefield temperature and the GSHP's inlet temperature, and that dynamic electricity prices have a significant impact on the system's operation. 
