TI  - Generalised water flow rate control strategy for optimal part load operation of ground source heat pump systems
T2  - Applied Energy
VL  - 150
PY  - 2015
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2015
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.apenergy.2015.03.134
AB  - A control strategy was developed to predict optimal ground source heat pump water flow rates under part load operation. Using this strategy, optimal flow rates are calculated from available design data and minimal on-line measurements. The optimal control strategy was evaluated with validated single speed and tandem speed ground source heat pump simulation models in both heating and cooling mode. A range of building load profiles were implemented when analysing heat pump system performance. The optimal strategy was shown to result in an improvement in system performance when compared to nominal flow rate operation, particularly for low load conditions. Due to the prevalence of low load operation over a heating or cooling season, the seasonal system performance (SPF3) was shown to increase by between 20% and 40% when using the optimal strategy.
KW  - coefficient of performance
KW  - Optimal
KW  - COP3
KW  - Control
KW  - Ground source heat pumps
KW  - control strategy
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. The optimal control strategy is analysed in quasi-steady state using the building simulation models. 
N1  - C2. The developed control strategy predicts steady state optimal internal and external circuit water flow rate, which is used to estimate optimal part load flow rate depending on current load conditions. 
N1  - C3. In steady state, the optimal control strategy results in COP3 values close to the simulated maximum over a range of tested part loads. In both steady and quasi-steady state the optimal strategy produces a significant improvement in COP3 when compared to nominal pump frequency operation, for low part load conditions. 
N1  - C4. This translates to an increase in SPF3 of over 20% in heating and 40% in cooling when the optimal control strategy is implemented over nominal frequency operation, as the system experiences low part load conditions for the majority of a season. 
N1  - C5. This simulation study demonstrates the performance improvements possible using the optimal flow rate control strategy, which will be evaluated in future work through experimental trials. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. Due to the prevalence of low load operation over a heating or cooling season, the seasonal system performance (SPF3) was shown to increase by between 20% and 40% when using the optimal strategy. 
N1  - D2. The Carnot efficiency can be affected by up to 1% per K, giving kEF a value of À0.01 or less. 
N1  - D3. The reduction in COP3 from the simulated maximum is less than 1% when using the optimal strategy. 
N1  - D4. COP3 decreases significantly, by up to 60%, when using the nominal frequency for decreasing part loads. 
N1  - D5. flow control is implemented over the nominal pump frequency. This increases to 38% and 40% for the single and tandem speed heat pumps in cooling mode. 
N1  - D6. However, in steady state, the optimal control strategy results in COP3 values close to the simulated maximum over a range of tested part loads. In both steady and quasi-steady state the optimal strategy produces a significant improvement in COP3 when compared to nominal pump frequency operation, particularly for low part load conditions. This translates to an increase in SPF3 of over 20% in heating and 40% in cooling when the optimal control strategy is implemented over nominal frequency operation, as the system experiences low part load conditions for the majority of a season. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The optimal flow rate control strategy for heat pumps is evaluated, showing significant improvements in COP3 and SPF3 compared to nominal pump frequency operation, particularly for low part load conditions.. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The key findings of the study include the development of a control strategy that can predict optimal ground source heat pump water flow rates under part load operation, resulting in an improvement in system performance of between 20% and 40%. The study also found that the optimal flow rate varies non-linearly with capacity, and that the internal and external flow rates can be optimized independently while still achieving system optimization.. 
N1  - E5. The optimal internal water flow rate decreases as the part load decreases, while the optimal external water flow rate remains relatively constant. The COP at part load conditions can be calculated using equations that take into account the steady state performance, power fraction, and capacity fraction.. 
N1  - E6. The key findings include the optimal control strategy resulting in COP3 values close to the simulated maximum over a range of tested part loads, and significant improvements in SPF3 of over 20% in heating and 40% in cooling when compared to nominal pump frequency operation.. 
N1  - E7. This translates to an increase in SPF3 of over 20% in heating and 40% in cooling when the optimal control strategy is implemented over nominal frequency operation, as the system experiences low part load conditions for the majority of a season. 
N1  - E8. This simulation study demonstrates the performance improvements possible using the optimal flow rate control strategy, which will be evaluated in future work through experimental trials. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The objective of the study was to develop a control strategy for optimizing internal and external water flow rate under part load conditions, with the goal of improving the seasonal performance of ground source heat pump systems. The study aimed to address the issue of optimal water flow control for fixed speed heat pump systems under part load conditions, which had received little attention in previous research.. 
N1  - E11. Methods. 
N1  - E12. The study used a combination of analytical and empirical methods to develop a simulation model of a ground source heat pump system. The study also used regression fitting techniques to generate multi-variant correlation curves, and combined the component models into quasi-steady state system models in MATLAB. The study evaluated the control strategy using validated simulation models of single speed and tandem speed ground source heat pump systems in both heating and cooling mode.. 
N1  - E13. The study uses a combination of theoretical analysis and simulation to derive the analytical equations for calculating the optimal internal and external water flow rates. The equations are based on the steady state performance, power fraction, and capacity fraction, and take into account the part load, internal water flow rate, and design parameters.. 
N1  - E14. The methods used include simulations using building simulation models, comparing the optimal flow rate control strategy to nominal pump frequency operation, and evaluating the results in terms of COP3 and SPF3.. 
N1  - E15. Results. 
N1  - E16. The results of the study showed that the optimal control strategy resulted in an improvement in system performance when compared to nominal flow rate operation, particularly for low load conditions. The study found that the seasonal system performance (SPF3) increased by between 20% and 40% when using the optimal strategy. The study also found that the internal and external flow rates can be optimized independently while still achieving system optimization.. 
N1  - E17. The study shows that the optimal internal water flow rate can be calculated using equations that take into account the part load, internal water flow rate, and design parameters. The results also show that the optimal external water flow rate remains relatively constant, and can be adjusted linearly between the estimated optimal 'mode 1' and 'mode 2' steady state flow rate as the part load moves from 0.5 to 1.. 
N1  - E18. The results show that the optimal control strategy results in COP3 values close to the simulated maximum over a range of tested part loads, and significant improvements in SPF3 of over 20% in heating and 40% in cooling when compared to nominal pump frequency operation.. 
N1  - E19. Conclusions. 
N1  - E20. The study concluded that the developed control strategy can predict optimal ground source heat pump water flow rates under part load operation, resulting in an improvement in system performance of between 20% and 40%. The study also concluded that the optimal flow rate varies non-linearly with capacity, and that the internal and external flow rates can be optimized independently while still achieving system optimization.. 
N1  - E21. The study concludes that the optimal internal and external water flow rates for a heat pump system can be determined using analytical equations, which can help maximize the COP at part load conditions. The results can be used to develop a control strategy for optimizing the performance of heat pump systems.. 
N1  - E22. The conclusions drawn from the study are that the optimal flow rate control strategy shows significant improvements in COP3 and SPF3 compared to nominal pump frequency operation, particularly for low part load conditions, and that this strategy has the potential to be implemented in real-world applications. 
