AB  - This paper is concerned with the development of a mathematical model, capable of describing the quasisteady state performance of an integrated ground source heat pump, which is used for heating and cooling of an institutional building located in a Mediterranean climate. The model is structured on functional basis according to the heat pump vapour compression or primary circuit, a secondary ground loop circuit and a secondary building loop circuit. Heat pump heating and cooling capacities, as well as COP, are considered to be dependent variables and are estimated in the model using performance fitted maps. Independent variables include: compressor speed, circulation pump speeds, ground loop return temperature and building circuit return temperature. The model is validated using data from a full-scale ground source heat pump installation. The validated model is used to examine system capacity and performance sensitivity to different control optimisation strategies, including set-point control of room air temperature, room air bandwidth temperature, building loop return water temperature and building loop return bandwidth temperature.
KW  - Ground source heat pump
KW  - Mathematical model
KW  - Engineering Equation Solver
KW  - steady state
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. Research concerning control issues related to vapour compression systems has largely focused on refrigeration and air-conditioning and to a lesser extent on heat pumps. 
N1  - C2. This paper examines, for a ground source heat pump (GSHP), how control of building circuit variables affects overall system performance. 
N1  - C3. A system level mathematical model was developed from the GSHP heat pump system using Engineering Equation Solver (EES) [17]. 
N1  - C4. The vapour compression software package IMST-ART was used to model the behaviour of the GSHP as a standalone system [18]. 
N1  - C5. This paper examines, for ground source heat pumps, how control of building circuit variables affects overall system performance. 
N1  - C6. It would appear that higher water return temperatures reduces the ON time operation of the heat pump, and results in an increase of daily heat pump seasonal performance factor (SPF) of approximately 5% per each degree increase of the building water return temperature. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. As can be observed on the right hand axes of Figs. 3–5, experimental measurements for cooling capacity and power input are predicted by the polynomial correlations with a maximum mean deviation better than 5% for cooling capacity and 2.4% for power input. 
N1  - D2. Analysis shows that capacity increases by approximately 3.6% per degree increase of the building return water temperature. 
N1  - D3. Values for the SPF of the system and the heat pump are affected by less than 0.5% by return water temperature bandwidth. 
N1  - D4. Values for the SPF of the system and the heat pump are slightly affected, less than 1%, by space temperature bandwidth. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. This paper presents a quasi-steady state mathematical model of an integrated ground source heat pump for building space control, which is validated using data from a full-scale ground source heat pump installation and used to examine system capacity and performance sensitivity to different control optimisation strategies.. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The model is validated using data from a full-scale ground source heat pump installation and is used to examine system capacity and performance sensitivity to different control optimisation strategies. The study finds that the ground source heat pump system offers relatively constant operating parameters for short-term measurements, insuring a smaller temperature difference between the condenser and evaporator.. 
N1  - E5. The study found that the developed mathematical model accurately predicted the GSHP system's performance, with a maximum mean deviation of 5% for cooling capacity and 2.4% for power input in cooling mode, and 13.8% for heating mode. The model also identified the importance of considering the thermal inertia of the evaporator and the hydronic loop in the system's performance.. 
N1  - E6. The key findings of the study include the significant impact of space set-point temperature on system power consumption, with a decrease in power consumption as the set-point temperature increases. Additionally, the study found that building water return temperature has a lesser influence on system power consumption, but still results in a decrease in compressor power consumption and an increase in daily heat pump SPF as the water return temperature increases.. 
N1  - E7. There are no key findings mentioned in the text regarding the influence of factors on overall system performance.. 
N1  - E8. It would appear that higher water return temperatures reduces the ON time operation of the heat pump, and results in an increase of daily heat pump seasonal performance factor (SPF) of approximately 5% per each degree increase of the building water return temperature. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The objective of the study is to develop a mathematical model of an integrated ground source heat pump for building space control and to examine system capacity and performance sensitivity to different control optimisation strategies. The study aims to investigate the role of various building circuit variables on the overall system performance.. 
N1  - E11. Methods. 
N1  - E12. The study uses a system level mathematical model developed from the GSHP heat pump system using Engineering Equation Solver (EES). The vapour compression software package IMST-ART is used to model the behaviour of the GSHP as a standalone system. The model is validated using experimental data from the full-scale ground source heat pump installation.. 
N1  - E13. The study used a combination of experimental and modeling approaches. The experimental data was used to develop correlations for heating and cooling mode, and to validate the mathematical model. The mathematical model was developed using Engineering Equation Solver and included sub-models for the heat pump, fan coils, circulation pumps, hydronic pipe network, storage tank, and building conditioned space.. 
N1  - E14. The study uses a system mathematical model developed using Engineering Equation Solver to assess the impact of various building circuit variables on ground source heat pump performance.. 
N1  - E15. Results. 
N1  - E16. The study finds that the validated model can be used to examine system capacity and performance sensitivity to different control optimisation strategies. The results show that the ground source heat pump system offers relatively constant operating parameters for short-term measurements.. 
N1  - E17. The study found that the developed mathematical model accurately predicted the GSHP system's performance, with a maximum mean deviation of 5% for cooling capacity and 2.4% for power input in cooling mode, and 13.8% for heating mode. The model also identified the importance of considering the thermal inertia of the evaporator and the hydronic loop in the system's performance.. 
N1  - E18. The results of the study show that space set-point temperature has the most significant effect on system power consumption, with a decrease in power consumption as the set-point temperature increases. The results also show that building water return temperature has a lesser influence on system power consumption, but still results in a decrease in compressor power consumption and an increase in daily heat pump SPF as the water return temperature increases.. 
N1  - E19. There are no results mentioned in the text regarding the influence of factors on overall system performance.. 
N1  - E20. Conclusions. 
N1  - E21. The study concludes that the developed mathematical model can be used to examine system capacity and performance sensitivity to different control optimisation strategies. The study highlights the importance of considering the role of various building circuit variables on the overall system performance.. 
N1  - E22. The study concluded that the developed mathematical model is a useful tool for optimizing and controlling GSHP systems. The model can be used to predict the system's performance and identify areas for improvement.. 
N1  - E23. The study concludes that space set-point temperature has the most significant effect on system power consumption, and that building water return temperature has a lesser influence on system power consumption. The study also concludes that building water return bandwidth and space temperature bandwidth have a negligible effect on system performance.. 
N1  - E24. The conclusions that can be drawn from the text are limited, as the influence of certain factors on overall system performance was not explored. 
