AB  - The aim of this paper is to present an optimal control algorithm to manage borehole thermal energy storage systems (BTES). Such a system gets exhausted, if it is employed intensively, i.e. outlet fluid is outside acceptable temperature ranges, and can no longer provide the desired heat or cold. To avoid this problem a control algorithm is proposed, which simultaneously optimizes the operation costs. This algorithm is based on a dynamic programming technique and results in an array which provides the optimal heat flux for a given field temperature, date and demand. The controller is illustrated on a simulation of an existing building. Several weather scenarios have been examined and the system remained robust under all situations.
KW  - Optimal control
KW  - dynamic programming
KW  - HVAC
KW  - heat flux
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. The aim of this paper is to present an optimal control algorithm to manage borehole thermal energy storage systems (BTES). 
N1  - C2. Such a system gets exhausted, if it is employed intensively, i.e. outlet fluid is outside acceptable temperature ranges, and can no longer provide the desired heat or cold. To avoid this problem a control algorithm is proposed, which simultaneously optimizes the operation costs. This algorithm is based on a dynamic programming technique and results in an array which provides the optimal heat flux for a given field temperature, date and demand. 
N1  - C3. A dynamic programming controller is able to operate the system within a given set of constraints. 
N1  - C4. The uncontrolled BTES installation operates at a lower cost. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. In most buildings more than 40% of the energy consumed is used for heating and cooling purposes. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The study presents a dynamic programming approach to optimize the control of a ground heat storage system, taking into account various constraints such as pump capacity, temperature limits, and heat supply demands, to minimize costs and ensure a reliable heat and cold supply.. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The optimal control algorithm is based on a dynamic programming technique and results in an array that provides the optimal heat flux for a given field temperature, date, and demand. The algorithm can cope with weather uncertainty and allows for off-line computations, facilitating the actual installation of the controller.. 
N1  - E5. The study finds that the dynamic programming approach can effectively optimize the control of the ground heat storage system, taking into account various constraints such as pump capacity, temperature limits, and heat supply demands. The study also finds that the optimal control policy depends on the temperature of the field, the demand for heat or cold, and the time of year. The study concludes that the dynamic programming approach can help to minimize costs and ensure a reliable heat and cold supply.. 
N1  - E6. Such a system gets exhausted, if it is employed intensively, i.e. outlet fluid is outside acceptable temperature ranges, and can no longer provide the desired heat or cold. To avoid this problem a control algorithm is proposed, which simultaneously optimizes the operation costs. This algorithm is based on a dynamic programming technique and results in an array which provides the optimal heat flux for a given field temperature, date and demand. 
N1  - E7. A dynamic programming controller is able to operate the system within a given set of constraints. 
N1  - E8. The uncontrolled BTES installation operates at a lower cost. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The objective of the study is to optimize the control of a ground heat storage system to minimize costs and ensure a reliable heat and cold supply. The study aims to develop a dynamic programming approach that takes into account various constraints such as pump capacity, temperature limits, and heat supply demands. The study also aims to evaluate the performance of the dynamic programming approach and compare it with other control strategies.. 
N1  - E11. Methods. 
N1  - E12. The algorithm uses a dynamic programming approach to optimize the operation of the borehole thermal energy storage system. A first-order response model is used to describe the dynamics of the underground storage system.. 
N1  - E13. The study uses a dynamic programming approach to optimize the control of the ground heat storage system. The approach involves discretizing time into weekly steps and dividing the demand and delivered heat or cold into intervals. The study also uses a cost function to evaluate the performance of the control policy. The cost function takes into account the cost of operating the system, including the cost of energy and maintenance.. 
N1  - E14. The study uses the TRNSYS simulation environment combined with the DST module to simulate a real underground heat storage field. The study also employs a dynamic programming approach to develop the controller. The study considers several scenarios, including mild winter and summer, normal winter and summer, and cold winter and hot summer.. 
N1  - E15. Results. 
N1  - E16. The algorithm allows for efficient heating and cooling of buildings, reducing the operation costs and avoiding exhausting the system. The system remained robust under all weather scenarios examined.. 
N1  - E17. The study finds that the dynamic programming approach can effectively optimize the control of the ground heat storage system. The study presents the results of the dynamic programming approach, including the optimal control policy and the cost function. The study also presents the evolution of the cost function over time.. 
N1  - E18. Conclusions. 
N1  - E19. The optimal control algorithm for borehole thermal energy storage systems can efficiently manage the operation costs and avoid exhausting the system, allowing for efficient heating and cooling of buildings. The algorithm can be used to reduce the investment cost of the system while guaranteeing the supply of needed heat and cold.. 
N1  - E20. The study concludes that the dynamic programming approach can help to minimize costs and ensure a reliable heat and cold supply. The study also concludes that the optimal control policy depends on the temperature of the field, the demand for heat or cold, and the time of year. The study recommends the use of the dynamic programming approach for optimizing the control of ground heat storage systems. 
