TI  - Flow rate control in standing column wells: A flexible solution for reducing the energy use and peak power demand of the built environment
T2  - Applied Energy
VL  - 313
PY  - 2022
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2022
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.apenergy.2022.118774
AB  - Standing column wells offer a generous and flexible solution to improve the energy efficiency of the built environment. As these systems behavior is strongly affected by groundwater advection, the acute challenge of predicting their thermal evolution with both accuracy and computational efficiency has so far hindered the development of design tools and optimized control strategies. In this work, a powerful simulation algorithm is implemented and applied to the performance assessment of multi-borehole standing column well systems located in a heterogeneous geological environment and operating under various constant and dynamic flow rate control strategies. The iterative algorithm relies on the non-stationary convolution technique to simulate the underground components, and the EnergyPlus approach to represent the heat pump efficiency at full and part loads. The findings suggest that using higher flow rates in peak conditions is a key element that minimizes auxiliary assistance and power demand. Complementary variable flow rate control aiming to maintain a 2 ◦C temperature difference across the plate heat exchanger has shown to alleviate groundwater usage and generate at least 8%–11% annual energy savings compared with constant flow. This operating strategy allowed the systems to deliver 197–246 W/m with a heating seasonal performance factor of 3.59. These results were achieved through 34 annual simulations having hourly time steps that were performed with the proposed algorithm in a total of 4 h 17 min, compared to 11 days for a single simulation using a numerical reference model.
KW  - Standing column well
KW  - Simulation
KW  - ground heat exchanger
KW  - Ground-source heat pump
KW  - Power demand
N1  - B. Participants: 4 performance indicators. 
N1  - B1. A detailed analysis of Case 4b with 5-SCWs is presented. This case is selected as it provides close to the best results obtained on all four performance indicators, namely the annual energy consumption (71 MWh), maximum power demand (35 kW), operating costs (9,799$). and groundwater usage (23 ML). 
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. A powerful simulation algorithm is implemented and applied to the performance assessment of multi-borehole standing column well systems located in a heterogeneous geological environment and operating under various constant and dynamic flow rate control strategies. 
N1  - C2. The results of several annual simulations comparing various flow rate control strategies are presented to illustrate the beneficial effect of variable flow rate control for simultaneously reducing the systems’ energy consumption, power demand, operating costs and groundwater usage. 
N1  - C3. The results demonstrate that variable flow rate control aiming to maintain a 2 °C fluid temperature difference across the heat pump leads to an increased energy efficiency, nearzero auxiliary solicitation and 35 kW of peak power usage instead of the 131 kW that would require an electric resistance heating system having a 100% efficiency. 
N1  - C4. We proposed a powerful simulation algorithm allowing the fast computation of the operating temperatures and performance indicators of a standing column well (SCW) system. 
N1  - C5. We showed that this flexible modeling strategy allows considering important contributors to SCWs thermal efficiency, namely the presence of a multi-layered geological environment having heterogeneous hydraulic and thermal conductivity distributions, and the use of a variable flow rate control strategy. 
N1  - C6. ground-source heat pump (GSHP) have maintained an annual growth rate of around 10% over the last ten years and reached a 77.5 GWt worldwide installed capacity as of 2020 [3]. 
N1  - C7. These results reveal the potential of the proposed simulation algorithm for future design purposes and developing innovative control strategies, leading to improved performance of SCW systems. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. GSHPs have maintained an annual growth rate of around 10% over the last ten years and reached a 77.5 GWt worldwide installed capacity as of 2020 [3]. 
N1  - D2. Montagud et al [9] estimated that this strategy can lead up to 28% reductions in annual energy consumption. 
N1  - D3. Beurcq [13, 14] obtained similar results in a rare numerical study investigating the influence of control strategies on the operation of SCWs in cold climates. They demonstrated the interest of using a variable-speed submersible pump to control the pumping and discharge rates linearly as a function of the load on the building loop, with energy and costs savings going up to 20% compared to good practice scenarios. 
N1  - D4. In Case 3, the discharge ratio is increased at 30% for the highest flow rate level, in order to maximize the system’s efficiency during peak demand periods. 
N1  - D5. The process is repeated iteratively until the correction on the ground loads is less than 0.75% of the maximal load. 
N1  - D6. Note that the discharge flow rates increase from g1 to g8. The difference between both systems appears important at lower discharge ratios (g1 and g2 have ratios of 0% and 10%, respectively), while it gradually diminishes as this parameter increases (20% for g3 to g7, and 30% for g8). 
N1  - D7. The dynamic flow rate cases show more steady and resilient results, and use up to 11% (4-SCWs) and 8% (5-SCWs) less energy annually than the best constant flow rate scenarios. 
N1  - D8. Comparison of Case 3b with 4 SCWs and Case 4b with 5 SCWs, which are very similar in terms of annual energy use, peak demand and operating costs, also suggests that the better efficiency of the 5-SCWs system at lower discharge flow rates would allow reducing the annual volume of discharged groundwater by 32% compared with the 4-SCWs. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The study presents a simulation-based analysis of the energy performance of a water-to-water heat pump system using standing column wells (SCWs) for a school building, evaluating the impact of variable flow rate control on energy consumption, power demand, operating costs, and groundwater usage.. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The key findings of the study suggest that using higher flow rates in peak conditions minimizes auxiliary assistance and power demand,. Additionally, complementary variable flow rate control aiming to maintain a 2 ◦C temperature difference across the plate heat exchanger can alleviate groundwater usage and generate at least 8%–11% annual energy savings,.. 
N1  - E5. The study identifies three main geological units on the study site, including unconsolidated deposits, sandy dolomite, and quartz arenite, with a constant undisturbed ground temperature of 8.5 °C observed down to a 133 m depth. The water table static level is found to be at a 2.5 m depth, and a 0.006 m/m hydraulic gradient is observed on the site.. 
N1  - E6. The study finds that variable flow rate control can reduce the system's energy consumption, power demand, operating costs, and groundwater usage, with the most efficient scenarios using dynamic flow rate control showing up to 11% less energy consumption annually than the best constant flow rate scenarios.. 
N1  - E7. The key findings of the study include the achievement of annual energy consumption of 71-76 MWh, operating costs of $9,751-$10,143, and a reduction in groundwater usage by 32% when using a 5-SCW system compared to a 4-SCW system.. 
N1  - E8. These results reveal the potential of the proposed simulation algorithm for future design purposes and developing innovative control strategies, leading to improved performance of SCW systems. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The objective of the study is to investigate the performance of a SCW system for heating and cooling, with a focus on minimizing energy use and cost. The study aims to evaluate the thermal behavior and performance of the SCW system, considering various control strategies involving constant and variable flow rates.. 
N1  - E11. Methods. 
N1  - E12. The study uses an efficient iterative algorithm that relies on the non-stationary convolution technique to simulate the underground components and the EnergyPlus approach to represent the effect of temperature and fluid flow rate on the heat pump’s efficiency,. Thirty-four annual simulations having hourly time steps are conducted to assess the performance of a SCW system operating under various constant and dynamic flow rate control strategies,.. 
N1  - E13. The study uses a simulation algorithm to evaluate the thermal behavior and performance of the SCW system, with a modeling strategy that involves using a response model to evaluate the transfer functions that represent the normalized thermal behavior of the borehole fields. The study also uses finite element models to evaluate the transfer functions, coupling heat transfer to Darcian groundwater flow, and considering the hydraulic and thermal interactions between the boreholes.. 
N1  - E14. The study uses a simulation-based approach, with the system modeled using the EnergyPlus approach, and the energy performance evaluated using a set of equations that take into account the thermal loads of the building, the ground loads, the work of the compressor, fluid pumps, and auxiliary equipment, as well as the pumping and circulation flow rates and the entering and leaving fluid temperature at the heat pump on the source side.. 
N1  - E15. The study used a simulation algorithm that relies on the non-stationary convolution technique to simulate the system's underground components, and the EnergyPlus approach to represent the temperature and flow rate dependency of the heat pump's efficiency.. 
N1  - E16. Results. 
N1  - E17. The results of the study show that the proposed algorithm can achieve fast simulation of SCWs operating under dynamic flow rate control,. The results also demonstrate the potential of flow rate control to improve the performance of SCWs, with energy savings of at least 8%–11% compared to constant flow,.. 
N1  - E18. The study results show that the SCW system can be designed to cover 80% of the peak power demand for heating, with an auxiliary electric resistance heating system to balance out the remaining loads. The study also investigates the effect of different control strategies on the system's performance, including constant and variable flow rates, and discharge flow rates.. 
N1  - E19. The study presents a comprehensive overview of the system's annual energy consumption, peak power demand, operating costs, and groundwater usage, with the results showing that variable flow rate control can reduce the system's energy consumption, power demand, operating costs, and groundwater usage.. 
N1  - E20. The results of the study showed that the SCW system achieved annual energy consumption of 71-76 MWh, operating costs of $9,751-$10,143, and a reduction in groundwater usage by 32% when using a 5-SCW system.. 
N1  - E21. Conclusions. 
N1  - E22. The study concludes that the proposed algorithm is a powerful tool for simulating SCWs and evaluating the impact of flow rate control strategies on their performance,. The study also highlights the potential of SCWs as a flexible solution for reducing energy use and peak power demand in the built environment,.. 
N1  - E23. The study concludes that the SCW system can be an effective solution for heating and cooling, with a focus on minimizing energy use and cost. The study highlights the importance of considering the geological units and groundwater flow regime in the design and operation of the SCW system.. 
N1  - E24. The study concludes that variable flow rate control is a cost-effective solution that allows minimizing both the energy consumption and power usage of the system, with the most efficient scenarios using dynamic flow rate control showing up to 11% less energy consumption annually than the best constant flow rate scenarios.. 
N1  - E25. The study concluded that SCW systems are flexible and can achieve high thermal efficiency due to their ability to work with conductive and advective heat transfer, and that an adapted variable flow rate control strategy can take advantage of these features to ensure the system's performance. 
