TI  - Capacity control in air–water heat pumps: Total cost of ownership analysis
T2  - Energy and Buildings
VL  - 81
PY  - 2014
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2014
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.enbuild.2014.06.029
AB  - Adjusting capacity to changing demand by variable speed control is known to offer efficiency improvement over classical on/off control. With a total cost of ownership analysis the economic viability of both control schemes is assessed for residential air–water heat pumps operating in different climate zones. Component sizes are optimized for both control methods individually. Results show optimal compressor displacement volumes to be smaller for variable speed than for on/off control. The optimal ratio of evaporator to condenser size is smaller for the variable speed system. Variable speed control is shown to be uneconomic for space heating in warmer climate while for average climate cost-effectiveness depends on the economic framework. For colder climate variable speed control is the more profitable choice in all considered cases; savings of up to 5000 EUR compared to on/off control can be achieved within 15 years of operation.
KW  - Heat pump
KW  - climate zone
KW  - total cost of ownership
KW  - Optimization
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. Changes in the control scheme can reduce emissions associated with air source heat pumps operating over a wide range of conditions by more than a third [1]. 
N1  - C2. The gradient of the curves steepen, when electric heat is added. This occurs for the fixed speed on/off (FS) system below balance point temperature Ta,b and for variable speed (VS) control below the upper speed limit temperature Ta,u. 
N1  - C3. The general trend for differences in total cost of ownership (TCO) is not reversed: In the warm climate zone VS control is too costly, in the average climate zone the benefit depends on the economic framework and variable speed control pays off in the colder climate. 
N1  - C4. It can be assumed that effects of points above and below the linear curve will outbalance each other in an economic comparison of fixed and variable speed control. 
N1  - C5. Variable speed units optimized for colder climates are slightly more cost efficient when run in the average climate than fixed speed units optimized for colder climates. 
N1  - C6. In the warmer climate zone the most cost efficient option is a fixed speed unit optimized for the conditions encountered there. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. The performance of the FS unit with electrical backup heat covering less than 5% of the annual demand was the same as the performance of the VS unit. 
N1  - D2. Deviations between seasonal power consumption calculated by Eq (3) and by the quadratic model (Eq (4)) are below 0.5% within the design region. 
N1  - D3. At higher ambient temperatures isentropic efficiency is up to 8% higher for FS operation. 
N1  - D4. Within the given size limits a maximum TCO increase of about 12% can be observed for undersized heat exchangers. 
N1  - D5. • No up to date values for prices of speed control of compressor and fan were available. 50% higher costs than assumed in this study increase payback time to 4 years for colder and 11 years for average climate (0.21 EUR kWh−1, interest rate 3%). 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The study compares the economic performance of fixed speed (FS) and variable speed (VS) control in air-water heat pumps, finding that VS control is most promising for colder climate zones, with a decrease in total cost of ownership (TCO) difference of about 20% when the cycle degradation coefficient is increased from 0.9 to 0.95.. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The study finds that variable speed control can be more profitable in colder climates, with savings of up to 5000 EUR compared to on/off control within 15 years of operation, and that optimal compressor displacement volumes are smaller for variable speed than for on/off control.. 
N1  - E5. The key findings of the study are that VS control reduces operating costs of the heat pump below the balance point temperature of the FS system, but above this temperature, the operating costs for the VS system become higher than for on/off control. The study also finds that the optimal component selection, such as compressor size and heat exchanger size, varies depending on the climate zone and control mode.. 
N1  - E6. The study finds that VS control lowers operating costs mainly by reducing electricity consumption at low ambient temperatures. The optimal component sizes differ for FS and VS control, with the optimized VS unit still requiring electric backup heating. The study also finds that the benefit of VS control is higher in colder climate zones, where the unit operates constantly, resulting in slower frost growth.. 
N1  - E7. Variable speed units optimized for colder climates are slightly more cost efficient when run in the average climate than fixed speed units optimized for colder climates. 
N1  - E8. In the warmer climate zone the most cost efficient option is a fixed speed unit optimized for the conditions encountered there. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The objective of the study is to quantify the total cost of ownership (TCO) of VS and FS control in residential air-water heat pumps for different climate zones, including investment costs and operating costs throughout the economic lifetime.. 
N1  - E11. Methods. 
N1  - E12. The study uses a simulation model of a typical heat pump cycle, comprising submodels of the compressor, evaporator, condenser, and expansion device, and optimizes component sizes to give minimum TCO for both FS and VS systems.. 
N1  - E13. The study uses a quadratic model to describe the seasonal power consumption of the system as a function of the inner heat transfer area of the evaporator and condenser. The study also uses the Design of Experiment (DoE) theory to minimize the number of simulation runs required for regression of the quadratic models.. 
N1  - E14. The study uses simulations to compare the performance of FS and VS control, considering various system parameters and climate zones. The study also uses cycle equations and NTU correlations to model the heat transfer capacity of the evaporator and condenser. The study employs compressor polynomials to express nominal evaporator capacity and nominal compressor power consumption as a function of evaporation and condensation pressure.. 
N1  - E15. Results. 
N1  - E16. The study finds that variable speed control can reduce emissions associated with air source heat pumps operating over a wide range of conditions by more than a third, and that the optimal ratio of evaporator to condenser size is smaller for the variable speed system.. 
N1  - E17. The results of the study show that the TCO of the VS system is lower than that of the FS system in the colder climate zone, but higher in the warmer climate zone. The study also finds that the optimal heat exchanger size and compressor size vary depending on the climate zone and control mode.. 
N1  - E18. The study finds that VS control is most promising for colder climate zones, with a decrease in TCO difference of about 20% when the cycle degradation coefficient is increased from 0.9 to 0.95. The study also finds that the optimal component sizes differ for FS and VS control, with the optimized VS unit still requiring electric backup heating. The results show that the benefit of VS control is higher in colder climate zones, where the unit operates constantly, resulting in slower frost growth.. 
N1  - E19. Conclusions. 
N1  - E20. The study concludes that variable speed control can be a more profitable option for residential air-water heat pumps in colder climates, and that optimizing component sizes is crucial to minimize total cost of ownership.. 
N1  - E21. The conclusions of the study are that the VS system is cost-efficient in the colder climate zone, but not in the warmer climate zone. The study also concludes that the optimal component selection, such as compressor size and heat exchanger size, varies depending on the climate zone and control mode.. 
N1  - E22. The study concludes that VS control is most promising for colder climate zones, with a decrease in TCO difference of about 20% when the cycle degradation coefficient is increased from 0.9 to 0.95. The study also concludes that the optimal component sizes differ for FS and VS control, with the optimized VS unit still requiring electric backup heating. The study suggests that further research is required to quantify the effect of tap water heating on the benefit of VS control. 
