TY  - JOUR
AU  - Montagud, Carla 
AU  - Corberán, José M. 
AU  - Montero, Álvaro 
TI  - In situ optimization methodology for the water circulation pumps frequency of ground source heat pump systems
T2  - Energy and Buildings
VL  - 68
PY  - 2013
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2013
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.enbuild.2013.09.030
AB  - This paper presents an experimental study of the influence of the water circulation pumps frequency at the indoor and outdoor loops in the overall system performance of a ground source heat pump monitored plant, and describes a three steps new methodology to optimize the system energy performance and obtain the values of the optimal frequencies when ON/OFF regulation is employed. The first step consists of several experimental tests of pseudo-random sequence of frequency steps for both, internal and external circulation pumps, carried out during a single day. The second step is the characterization of the quasi-steady state system performance as a function of circulation pumps frequencies. The final step consists of, by means of an analytical expression, the extrapolation of results to any partial load ratio, what allows finding the optimal frequency as a function of the instantaneous thermal load. The advantage of the proposed methodology is that it can be carried out on site and is able to consider the phenomena occurring at the heat pump and the ground source heat exchanger when the flow rates are varied. The methodology can be applied to any installation incorporating variable speed circulation pumps. Results indicate large energy savings potential for this kind of installation.
KW  - Ground source heat pump
KW  - energy performance
KW  - Energy efficiency
KW  - valència
KW  - IDAE
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. According to data provided by IDAE [4], energy consumption in buildings in Spain accounts for 20% of final energy consumption, a figure that tends to increase. 
N1  - C2. The purpose of this paper is to examine the influence of the flow rate of the water circulation pumps on the overall performance of a ground source heat pump system for heating and cooling, as well as the optimization of their frequency in order to lead to the minimal energy consumption of the entire system. 
N1  - C3. Energy efficiency parameters are calculated from the power consumption readings and the heat transferred from the heat pump to the internal circuit, which is estimated from the values of TINIC, TOUTIC and the water flow rate m. 
N1  - C4. The first step consists in the experimental characterization of the system energy performance (COPSYSTEM) as a function of the circulation pump frequencies during one entire day for heating and one for cooling. 
N1  - C5. Further optimization is to be carried out in Geocool installation where appropriate control algorithms, including adequate control strategies to ensure users comfort will be implemented in order to optimize the daily energy performance of the system. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. The results show that there is a high potential for energy savings which has been estimated as 28% of annual electricity consumption for the studied case. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The study proposes a methodology for optimizing the frequency of water circulation pumps in a geothermal system with ON/OFF regulation, which consists of three steps: characterization of system performance, estimation of system COP maps, and estimation of system performance maps for any thermal load.. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The study found that optimizing the water circulation pumps frequency can lead to large energy savings, and that the higher the flow rate at both the external and internal circuit, the better the COP of the heat pump.. 
N1  - E5. The study reveals that the influence of water flow rates on the heat pump system is not linear, and there exists an optimum frequency for each water loop, with increasing water flow rates diminishing compressor consumption but increasing circulation pumps consumption.. 
N1  - E6. The study found that the optimal frequencies for the circulation pumps depend on the operating conditions, and that the system's performance can be improved by optimizing the pump frequencies. The study also found that the internal circulation pump consumption contributes to the heating, adding useful heat, while in cooling mode, it has the opposite effect.. 
N1  - E7. The study found that the optimal circulation pump frequencies vary depending on the load ratio, with lower frequencies resulting in lower optimal frequencies at lower loads. The optimal frequencies for heating and cooling modes were found to be different, with 40 Hz for the internal pump and 30 Hz for the external one in heating mode, and 30 Hz for the internal pump and 40 Hz for the external one in cooling mode.. 
N1  - E8. Further optimization is to be carried out in Geocool installation where appropriate control algorithms, including adequate control strategies to ensure users comfort will be implemented in order to optimize the daily energy performance of the system. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The purpose of the paper is to examine the influence of the flow rate of the water circulation pumps on the overall performance of a ground source heat pump system for heating and cooling, as well as the optimization of their frequency in order to lead to the minimal energy consumption of the entire system.. 
N1  - E11. Methods. 
N1  - E12. The study employed a three-step methodology: (1) several experimental tests of pseudo-random sequence of frequency steps for both internal and external circulation pumps, (2) characterization of the quasi-steady state system performance as a function of circulation pumps frequencies, and (3) extrapolation of results to any partial load ratio using an analytical expression.. 
N1  - E13. The study uses experimental and monitoring methods to analyze the performance of the heat pump system, including measuring water temperatures, flow rates, and power consumption of each system component.. 
N1  - E14. The study uses a quasi-steady state mathematical model of the installation, which was validated by comparison with experimental data. The study also employs an analytical methodology to estimate the system performance factor as a function of the partial load ratio.. 
N1  - E15. The methodology consists of three steps: experimental characterization of the system energy performance, quasi-steady state performance maps construction, and extrapolating the quasi-steady COPSYSTEM values to any load ratio. The study used experimental measurements and analytical expressions to develop the methodology.. 
N1  - E16. Results. 
N1  - E17. The results show that the higher the inverter frequency, the greater the circulating water flow rate, and that the power consumption typically increases in a cubic manner with flow rate.. 
N1  - E18. The results show that the compressor power consumption is the greatest, accounting for 83% of the total consumption, while the internal circulation pump consumption is relatively high, around 12% of the compressor power.. 
N1  - E19. The study found that the optimal frequencies for the circulation pumps depend on the operating conditions, and that the system's performance can be improved by optimizing the pump frequencies. The study also found that the internal circulation pump consumption contributes to the heating, adding useful heat, while in cooling mode, it has the opposite effect.. 
N1  - E20. The results show that the optimal frequencies for heating and cooling modes vary depending on the load ratio, with lower frequencies resulting in lower optimal frequencies at lower loads. The study estimated a potential annual energy savings of 28%.. 
N1  - E21. Conclusions. 
N1  - E22. The study concludes that optimizing the water circulation pumps frequency can lead to large energy savings, and that the proposed methodology can be applied to any installation incorporating variable speed circulation pumps.. 
N1  - E23. The study concludes that there is an optimum frequency for each water loop, and the energy efficiency parameters can be calculated from monitored data, allowing for the evaluation of the system's performance.. 
N1  - E24. The study concludes that the proposed methodology is effective in optimizing the frequency of water circulation pumps in a geothermal system with ON/OFF regulation. The study also concludes that the system's performance can be improved by optimizing the pump frequencies, taking into account the operating conditions and the system's characteristics. 
