AU  - Cervera-Vázquez, J. 
AU  - Montagud, Carla 
AU  - Corberán, José M. 
TI  - In situ optimization methodology for the water circulation pumps frequency of ground source heat pump systems: Analysis for multistage heat pump units
T2  - Energy and Buildings
VL  - 88
PY  - 2014
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2014
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.enbuild.2014.12.008
AB  - In order to optimize the global energy performance of a ground source heat pump system, special attention needs to be paid to the auxiliaries as they stand for a considerable part of the total energy consumption. A new in situ experimental methodology based on the frequency variation of the water circulation pumps in order to optimize the energy performance of the system was previously published by the authors for a ground source heat pump system using a single stage heat pump with ON/OFF regulation. The original single stage heat pump was recently replaced with a multistage unit consisting of two compressors of the same capacity working in tandem. A new experimental campaign was carried out and a new study was performed in order to adapt the in situ optimization methodology to the performance of the tandem compressors unit, and, by extension, to the multistage case. This paper presents the in situ optimization methodology for the water circulation pumps frequency adapted for multistage ground source heat pump systems. Results show that energy savings up to 32% can be obtained by applying this optimization methodology.
KW  - Ground source heat pump
KW  - energy performance
KW  - water circulation
KW  - Auxiliaries
N1  - B. Participants: 1 data. 
N1  - B1. Given that the analyzed heat pump consists of two compressors in tandem, the performance will be different whether there is one or there are two compressors running, i.e. only one compressor cycling ON/OFF or one compressor continuously running and the other one cycling ON/OFF. Therefore, step 1 tests must be carried out for both operation states (n = 1 and n = 2). For this particular installation, it has been experimentally confirmed that the optimum frequencies are located at higher values when both compressors are running (around 45–50 Hz), and hence there is no need for testing frequencies lower than 35 Hz. 
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. In the current context of global warming concern, special attention should be paid to the efficient use of renewable energies. 
N1  - C2. The authors previously developed an experimental in situ optimization methodology for the water circulation pumps frequency of ground source heat pump systems when single stage ON/OFF regulation is employed [12]. 
N1  - C3. This paper describes the in situ methodology to determine those optimum frequencies for a multistage heat pump unit. 
N1  - C4. When analyzing a multistage heat pump unit, which consists of a specific number of compressors (N) working in tandem, special attention should be paid to the thermal demand of the building, since it will determine the number of stages in operation and the compressor consumption. 
N1  - C5. The methodology previously developed and published by the authors for single stage heat pump units with ON/OFF regulation was adapted for multistage heat pump units. 
N1  - C6. Given that the in situ optimization methodology has been developed for both single stage and multistage heat pump units with ON/OFF regulation, further studies will focus on the adaptation of the methodology to Ground source heat pump (GSHP) installations with variable speed heat pump units. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. Results show that energy savings up to 32% can be obtained by applying this optimization methodology. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The study presents an in situ experimental methodology to optimize the circulation pumps frequency of a geothermal heat pump installation, leading to minimum energy consumption, by characterizing the performance of the system during the ON time of the compressor at any possible combination of frequencies.. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The key findings of the study include the development of an in situ optimization methodology for the water circulation pumps frequency of ground source heat pump systems with multistage heat pump units, and the determination of the optimal pair of frequencies for the water loops to achieve energy savings.. 
N1  - E5. The study found that the optimal frequencies are located at higher values when both compressors are running, and the performance maps of the unit can be obtained as a function of both the external and internal circulation pumps frequency.. 
N1  - E6. The study found that the optimization methodology resulted in energy savings of 29% for a single day and 31.70% for the entire heating season. The optimal frequencies for the circulation pumps were found to be higher for higher partial load ratios.. 
N1  - E7. The key findings of the study were that the optimized control system resulted in energy savings of 29.4% for a typical heating day and 31.70% over the entire analyzed period.. 
N1  - E8. Given that the in situ optimization methodology has been developed for both single stage and multistage heat pump units with ON/OFF regulation, further studies will focus on the adaptation of the methodology to Ground source heat pump (GSHP) installations with variable speed heat pump units. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The objective of the study is to optimize the energy performance of ground source heat pump systems with multistage heat pump units by developing an in situ optimization methodology for the water circulation pumps frequency.. 
N1  - E11. Methods. 
N1  - E12. The study uses an experimental approach, with a network of sensors set up to monitor the installation, including water temperatures, mass flow rates, and power consumptions.. 
N1  - E13. The methodology consists of three steps: (1) experimental tests of pseudorandom sequence of frequency steps for both internal and external circulation pumps, (2) analysis of the results and estimation of the system COP, and (3) extrapolation of the performance maps to any partial load ratio of the system.. 
N1  - E14. The methodology involved experimental tests to determine the optimal frequencies for the circulation pumps, which were then implemented in the control board of the heat pump unit. The tests were carried out for different partial load ratios and working modes (heating and cooling).. 
N1  - E15. The methods used in the study involved continuously monitoring the heat pump system over several months and comparing the optimized control to a standard control.. 
N1  - E16. Results. 
N1  - E17. The results of the study show that energy savings of up to 32% can be achieved by applying the optimization methodology, and that the optimal pair of frequencies for the water loops depends on the thermal demand of the building.. 
N1  - E18. The study obtained the system COP maps for one typical heating and one typical cooling day, and the optimal pair of frequencies corresponds to quasisteady state working conditions.. 
N1  - E19. The results showed that the optimization methodology resulted in energy savings and improved system performance. The optimal frequencies for the circulation pumps were found to be higher for higher partial load ratios.. 
N1  - E20. The results of the study showed that the optimized control system resulted in significant energy savings, with a 29.4% savings for a typical heating day and a 31.70% savings over the entire period.. 
N1  - E21. Conclusions. 
N1  - E22. The study concludes that the in situ optimization methodology for the water circulation pumps frequency of ground source heat pump systems with multistage heat pump units is effective in achieving energy savings, and that the optimal pair of frequencies for the water loops depends on the thermal demand of the building.. 
N1  - E23. The study concludes that the proposed methodology can be applied to any installation provided with a multistage heat pump unit with more than two compressors, and the system performance factor can be obtained as a function of the partial load ratio of each stage.. 
N1  - E24. The study concluded that the optimization methodology is effective in improving the energy efficiency of ground source heat pump systems with multistage heat pump units. The methodology can be used to optimize the performance of such systems and reduce energy consumption.. 
N1  - E25. The conclusions of the study were that the optimized control system is effective in reducing energy consumption for heat pump systems. 
