AU  - Capozza, Antonio 
AU  - Zarrella, Angelo 
AU  - De Carli, Michele 
TI  - Long-term analysis of two GSHP systems using validated numerical models and proposals to optimize the operating parameters
T2  - Energy and Buildings
VL  - 93
PY  - 2015
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2015
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.enbuild.2015.02.005
AB  - An important consideration with regard to ground source heat pump (GSHP) systems is ensuring high energy efficiency performance throughout the years. Multi-year analyses are thus exceedingly important to evaluate the seasonal oscillations in the ground temperature and its long-term drift.As only a limited number of results based on real cases are available in the literature, this work aims to address this paucity of information. Seasonal and long-term computer simulations were conducted in order to investigate the thermal performance of two office buildings in Italy with unbalanced load profiles. The study focuses on the evaluation of ground temperature drift which was essentially due to an imbalance of the buildings’ load profile.
KW  - Borehole
KW  - Heat pump
KW  - ground source heat pump
KW  - coefficient of performance
KW  - borehole heat exchangers
KW  - ASHRAE
KW  - Ground heat exchanger
N1  - B. Participants: 90 people. 
N1  - B1. Three floors are above the ground and one is underground (see Fig. 2a). Approximately 90 people work in the building. The north and south facades are completely glazed. 
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. Over recent decades, ground source heat pump (GSHP) systems, which are used worldwide for space heating and cooling of buildings, have been shown to be an attractive as well as promising technology [1]. 
N1  - C2. The main disadvantage of GSHP systems compared to conventional ones is higher initial costs which include those covering the geotechnical investigations and inspections on the ground and linked to the thermal-physical characteristics of groundwater as well as of drilling a borehole ground heat exchanger or wells to extract groundwater. 
N1  - C3. Using TRNSYS, Montagud et al [12] analyzed a complete GSHP system consisting of six vertical boreholes and compared the simulation results with experimental measurements for one operational day in the cooling mode of a reversible water-to-water heat pump of nominal heating and cooling capacities equal to 18 kW and 14 kW, respectively. 
N1  - C4. Great differences were instead found in carried out in the second building (Case B) with regard to the thermal performance of the GSHP system owing to the totally different design loads found during the monitoring activity. 
N1  - C5. From a strictly energetic viewpoint, an improvement in plant performance follows when the ratio between the heating and cooling loads to the heat pumps is rebalanced by hybrid GSHP systems, i.e. by auxiliary equipment improving the surplus cooling/heating loads: chillers in Case A, auxiliary heaters in Case B. 
N1  - C6. The energetic-economic evaluation did not justify adopting supplementary borehole heat exchangers (BHEs); load balancing could be appropriate if the auxiliary equipment was already installed in the plant and if its cost was negligible and lowered operating expenses. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. At optimum conditions, the average coefficient of performance (COP) of the hybrid GSHP system increased by approximately 7.2% compared with that of the pure one. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The study evaluates the performance of a ground source heat pump (GSHP) system, analyzing the effects of thermal drift and heat imbalance on the system's efficiency and operating costs, and compares different strategies to improve the system's performance, including load balancing and increasing the number of boreholes.. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The key findings of the study are that rebalancing the ratio between the heat extracted from and injected into the ground leads to better results from an energetic viewpoint, and that increasing the number of boreholes can reduce seasonal oscillations and thermal drift.. 
N1  - E5. The study uses different approaches, including the CaRM simulation tool and the ASHRAE method, to analyze the long-term thermal performance of the two GSHP systems. The results show that the ground heat loads were used as data in the simulations carried out using the CaRM tool.. 
N1  - E6. The key findings of the study include the significant impact of thermal drift on the long-term performance of GSHP systems, and the importance of considering ground heat load imbalance in system design, as well as the effectiveness of increasing the number of boreholes in reducing thermal drift.. 
N1  - E7. The study finds that reducing the heat imbalance on the ground side is the key factor to limiting the effect of the thermal drift, and that increasing the number of boreholes does not significantly affect the thermal drift. The study also finds that the ground temperature drift depends primarily on the annual heat imbalance between heating and cooling loads. Additionally, the study shows that load balancing can lead to lower electricity consumption of heat pumps than increasing the number of boreholes.. 
N1  - E8. The energetic-economic evaluation did not justify adopting supplementary borehole heat exchangers (BHEs); load balancing could be appropriate if the auxiliary equipment was already installed in the plant and if its cost was negligible and lowered operating expenses. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The objective of the study is to address the paucity of information on the long-term thermal behavior of ground source heat pump systems, and to evaluate the effects of improved load balancing and increased number of boreholes on the thermal performance of the systems.. 
N1  - E11. Methods. 
N1  - E12. The study uses seasonal and long-term computer simulations to investigate the thermal performance of the ground source heat pump systems, and compares the results to two analytical approaches available in the literature.. 
N1  - E13. The study uses a combination of numerical and analytical models to analyze the long-term thermal performance of the two GSHP systems. The CaRM simulation tool is used as the numerical approach, while the ASHRAE method and the infinite line source model are used as analytical approaches.. 
N1  - E14. The study uses a combination of simulation tools, including CaRM, and analysis procedures, such as the ASHRAE method, to evaluate the long-term thermal performance of the GSHP systems, and to assess the effects of thermal drift and ground heat load imbalance.. 
N1  - E15. Results. 
N1  - E16. The results of the study show that rebalancing the ratio between the heat extracted from and injected into the ground leads to better results from an energetic viewpoint, and that increasing the number of boreholes can reduce seasonal oscillations and thermal drift.. 
N1  - E17. The study finds that the ground temperature drift depends primarily on the annual heat imbalance between heating and cooling loads. The study also finds that reducing the heat imbalance on the ground side is the key factor to limiting the effect of the thermal drift. The results of the study are summarized in Tables 4, 5, and 6.. 
N1  - E18. Conclusions. 
N1  - E19. The conclusions of the study are that ground source heat pump systems can be an attractive and promising technology for space heating and cooling of buildings, but that careful design and optimization are necessary to ensure high energy efficiency performance.. 
N1  - E20. The study concludes that the use of different approaches, including numerical and analytical models, can help improve the design and operation of GSHP systems.. 
N1  - E21. The conclusions of the study highlight the importance of considering thermal drift and ground heat load imbalance in the design of GSHP systems, and the need for careful evaluation of system performance over the long term, using tools such as CaRM and ASHRAE procedures. 
