TI  - Extremum seeking control for efficient operation of hybrid ground source heat pump system
T2  - Renewable Energy
VL  - 86
PY  - 2015
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2015
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.renene.2015.07.092
AB  - The Hybrid Ground Source Heat Pump (GSHP) systems combine the renewable geothermal energy and cooling tower for rejecting the cooling load, which is often adopted for high cooling demand. Model based control can be limited due to variations in ambient conditions, ground-loop heat exchanger (GHE) and equipment characteristics, cost and reliability of sensors. A self-optimizing control scheme is proposed for efficient operation of the hybrid GSHP based on Extremum Seeking Control (ESC), with feedback of the total power consumption and the control inputs of the relative flow rate of cooling tower and the water pump speed. The cooling capacity of the heat pump regulates the evaporator leaving water at 7 C. A Modelica based dynamic simulation model is developed for a Hybrid GSHP system, with the vertical GHE model adopted from Modelica Buildings Library. The transient heat transfer is implemented with a finite volume method inside and outside the borehole. The proposed ESC scheme is evaluated under the scenarios of fixed cooling load, ramp change in the evaporator inlet water temperature, diurnal sinusoidal cycle of air wet-bulb temperature, and realistic ambient and cooling load condition. Simulation results show the proposed ESC strategy effectively achieves nearly optimal efficiency without the need for plant model.
KW  - Ground Source Heat Pump
KW  - ground heat exchanger
KW  - Modelica
KW  - power consumption
KW  - dynamic simulation
KW  - artificial neural networks
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. The Ground Source Heat Pump (GSHP) is a technology that utilizes the renewable geothermal energy for space heating and air conditioning of residential and commercial buildings, which has demonstrated higher energy efficiency for residential and commercial buildings [1]. 
N1  - C2. Entering the heat pump and performance degradation of the GSHP system [2]. This problem may be solved by increasing the total capacity of ground heat exchanger (GHE); the system capacity is limited by the initial cost of construction. 
N1  - C3. Li et al [30] used an Extremum Seeking Control (ESC) scheme for chilled-water system to look for the energy efficient operation and presented a Modelica based dynamic simulation model for demonstrating the effectiveness of the proposed control strategy. 
N1  - C4. This paper presents a multi-variable ESC based hybrid GSHP control scheme which can minimize the combined power consumption of GHE loop water pump, cooling tower fan and pump, and heat pump compressor. 
N1  - C5. The multi-variable ESC control strategy is tested on a dynamic simulation model of the hybrid GSHP system developed by utilizing the Modelica Buildings Library and TIL Library. 
N1  - C6. This study indicates significant potential for ESC to achieve good energy efficiency in real time with a model-free advantage. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. The multi-variable ESC controller is simulated for the hybrid GSHP system under 100% cooling load conditions. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The study presents a dynamic simulation model of a hybrid ground source heat pump (GSHP) system, which is developed using Dymola 2014, Modelica Buildings Library 1.5, and TIL Library 3.1.0, and a two-input extremum seeking control (ESC) scheme is proposed to optimize the system's performance.. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The proposed ESC scheme effectively achieves nearly optimal efficiency without the need for a plant model,. The study also finds that the ESC scheme requires minimal measurement, i.e., the total power measurement only, which can significantly reduce the cost of sensor acquisition, installation, and maintenance,.. 
N1  - E5. The study proposes a two-input ESC scheme to optimize the hybrid GSHP system's performance, which aims to minimize the total power consumption of the system. The ESC scheme uses the cooling-tower fan relative air-flow rate and the water pump flow rate as control inputs, and the total power consumption of the system as the feedback signal.. 
N1  - E6. The proposed multi-variable ESC scheme is validated with simulations, showing that it can achieve an energy saving of about 9.3% compared to constant-input operation. The scheme is also shown to be robust to sensor failure and plant variation due to unknown environment changes and system degradation.. 
N1  - E7. The multi-variable ESC control strategy is tested on a dynamic simulation model of the hybrid GSHP system developed by utilizing the Modelica Buildings Library and TIL Library. 
N1  - E8. This study indicates significant potential for ESC to achieve good energy efficiency in real time with a model-free advantage. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The objective of the study is to develop a self-optimizing control scheme for efficient operation of hybrid GSHP systems,. The study aims to address the issue of heat accumulation in the ground in cooling-dominated buildings,.. 
N1  - E11. Methods. 
N1  - E12. The study uses a Modelica-based dynamic simulation model to evaluate the proposed control strategy,. The model includes a ground heat exchanger (GHE) model, which is adopted from the Modelica Buildings Library,.. 
N1  - E13. The study uses Dymola 2014, Modelica Buildings Library 1.5, and TIL Library 3.1.0 to develop the dynamic simulation model of the hybrid GSHP system. The ESC scheme is designed using a multi-variable ESC design, which follows the guidelines provided in Ref. [42] [27].. 
N1  - E14. The study uses a dynamic simulation model of the hybrid GSHP system, developed using the Modelica Buildings Library and TIL Library. The model includes a transient heat conduction model of the vertical ground heat exchanger, a static polynomial cooling tower model, and a heat pump model. The multi-variable ESC scheme is designed and implemented using a two-input ESC framework, with the control inputs being the cooling-tower fan relative air-flow rate and the water pump flow rate.. 
N1  - E15. Results. 
N1  - E16. The simulation results show that the proposed ESC strategy effectively achieves nearly optimal efficiency without the need for a plant model,. The results also demonstrate that the ESC scheme can optimize the control inputs for each channel in real-time, without relying on the accurate model of the system,.. 
N1  - E17. The study presents the results of the ESC simulation under a fixed cooling load condition, which shows the trajectories of the superheat and expansion valve opening. The results demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed ESC scheme in optimizing the system's performance.. 
N1  - E18. Conclusions. 
N1  - E19. The study concludes that the proposed ESC scheme is an effective method for achieving efficient operation of hybrid GSHP systems,. The study also concludes that the ESC scheme requires minimal measurement, which can significantly reduce the cost of sensor acquisition, installation, and maintenance,.. 
N1  - E20. The study concludes that the proposed multi-variable ESC scheme is effective in optimizing the energy efficiency of a hybrid GSHP system. The scheme is shown to be robust to sensor failure and plant variation, and can achieve an energy saving of about 9.3% compared to constant-input operation. 
