TI  - Optimal control of fan coil battery air and water flow rates requiring minimal on-line measurements
T2  - Applied Thermal Engineering
VL  - 198
PY  - 2021
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2021
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.applthermaleng.2021.117469
AB  - Fan coil units are widely used in air-conditioning systems for heating and cooling of commercial buildings. Control - capable of achieving better operational efficiencies and at the same time, guarantying thermal comfort - is paramount in order to achieve optimal operation. The present paper presents a novel generalised control strategy, requiring only minimal input data, for optimising fan speed in order to reduce. Different control models are implemented to predict fan coil capacities and associated total power consumption. The developed strategy has been compared to fixed speed and benchmark fan speed control strategies, using both a steady state and a quasi-steady state algorithm, for various building loads. Results show average reduction in fan coil battery power consumption of 34% and 43% in heating and cooling mode respectively, when the optimal control is compared to fixed fan speed settings. Savings between 4.9% and 9.1% can be achieved by the control algorithms if compared to the benchmark fan speed control strategies.
KW  - ground-source heat pump
KW  - Fan coil units
KW  - Buildings
KW  - optimal control
KW  - Thermal comfort
KW  - air handling unit
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. The building sector is responsible for about 40% of the overall energy consumption and 36% of CO2 emissions worldwide. 
N1  - C2. Fan coils Fan coil unit Ground source heat pump Heating, ventilation and air Inlet Outlet Power, KW Thermal Power, KW real, KW Temperature, ◦C Water Change in water flow rate Change in fan coil capacity conditioning studies have focused on optimal control using minimal input data for small or medium capacity Fan coil units (FCU) systems working at partial loads. 
N1  - C3. Due to differences in fan coil battery design, separate strategies are developed for bypass and modulated valve fan coil battery sub-systems. 
N1  - C4. The optimal fan speed control strategy was developed through derivation and implementation of a fan coil capacity estimation equation and a circulation pump power control model. 
N1  - C5. The results showed that the optimisation strategy operates effectively under a range of loads and is applicable to different system designs requiring only readily available inputs. 
N1  - C6. At low water flow rates, the difference between experimental and numerical data is more pronounced, especially for the low air flow setting. This is due to the greater impact of systematic errors that are commonly exhibited at the lower flow ranges of the instrumentation. These differences are always less than 10%, which is considered acceptable as per ASHRAE standard [36] and other research [37,38], especially considering the simplified nature of the correlation adopted. 
N1  - C7. In large fan coil battery systems, such as the one examined the proposed approach has potential: (i) to ameliorate data error by using minimal instrumentation instances, (ii) to reduce associated instrument capital costs, and, (iii) to enhance system savings arising from the proposed algorithm. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. The water temperature set-point must be sufficient to ensure the load delivery, while maximising heat pump performance, which is highly dependent on temperature (e.g., decreasing the supply temperature, in heating mode, from 55 ◦C to 35 ◦C can increase the SPF by up to 40% [13]). 
N1  - D2. The minimum total power is located at a point where the rate of change in the pump power is equal to the rate of change in total fan power. In this case, by operating at the optimal combination of fan speeds, the total power consumption can be reduced by 13%, 10% and 30%, when compared to fixed fan speeds of 1, 2 and 3 in all zones, respectively. 
N1  - D3. At low water flow rates, the difference between experimental and numerical data is more pronounced, especially for the low air flow setting. This is due to the greater impact of systematic errors that are commonly exhibited at the lower flow ranges of the instrumentation. These differences are always less than 10%, which is considered acceptable as per ASHRAE standard [36] and other research [37,38], especially considering the simplified nature of the correlation adopted. 
N1  - D4. For all capacities, an average reduction in total power of 5.7% and 5.9% is observed when optimal fan speed control is compared with Cmin = Cmax control and design flow control, respectively. 
N1  - D5. The saving increases up to 29% when compared to a constant fan speed setting of 3 in all zones. 
N1  - D6. Optimal fan speed control results in a maximum reduction in energy usage of 7.9% and 9.1%, when compared to Cmin = Cmax control in heating and cooling, respectively. 
N1  - D7. A maximum reduction of 6.3% and 5.8% is observed when compared to design flow control in heating and cooling, respectively. 
N1  - D8. On average total energy is reduced by 34% when optimal fan speed control is used over maximum fan speed settings in each zone. 
N1  - D9. • Significant savings (up to 34% in heating mode and up to 43% in cooling mode) are reported when any of the fan speed control strategies are compared to fixed fan speed operation. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The present paper presents a novel generalised control strategy for optimising fan speed in fan coil units to reduce power consumption while maintaining thermal comfort, with results showing average reductions of 34% and 43% in heating and cooling modes respectively.. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The results show average reductions in fan coil battery power consumption of 34% and 43% in heating and cooling modes respectively, when the optimal control is compared to fixed fan speed settings. Savings between 4.9% and 9.1% can be achieved by the control algorithms if compared to the benchmark fan speed control strategies.. 
N1  - E5. The key findings include: optimal fan speed control results in minimum daily energy consumption for the bypass fan coil battery in heating mode, and savings of between 4.9% and 5.4% can be achieved compared to other control strategies. Additionally, significant savings of up to 34% in heating mode and up to 43% in cooling mode are reported when any of the fan speed control strategies are compared to fixed fan speed operation.. 
N1  - E6. The results showed that the optimisation strategy operates effectively under a range of loads and is applicable to different system designs requiring only readily available inputs. 
N1  - E7. At low water flow rates, the difference between experimental and numerical data is more pronounced, especially for the low air flow setting. This is due to the greater impact of systematic errors that are commonly exhibited at the lower flow ranges of the instrumentation. These differences are always less than 10%, which is considered acceptable as per ASHRAE standard [36] and other research [37,38], especially considering the simplified nature of the correlation adopted. 
N1  - E8. In large fan coil battery systems, such as the one examined the proposed approach has potential: (i) to ameliorate data error by using minimal instrumentation instances, (ii) to reduce associated instrument capital costs, and, (iii) to enhance system savings arising from the proposed algorithm. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The objective of the study is to develop and test a novel generalised control strategy for minimising power consumption of a fan coil battery at part load, requiring only minimal available input data. The goal is to maintain the operation within the zone temperature set-points and bandwidth while reducing power consumption.. 
N1  - E11. Methods. 
N1  - E12. The study uses a combination of simulation models and experimental data to develop and test the control strategy. The control strategy is based on a new correlation exploiting minimal data set, including water and air inlet temperatures and available fan coil battery nominal design data. The strategy is implemented in a simulation model to characterise the fan coil battery of a ground-source heat pump (GSHP) system.. 
N1  - E13. The methods used include steady state and quasi-steady state analyses of the control strategies, and the development of a fan coil capacity estimation equation and a circulation pump power control model.. 
N1  - E14. Results. 
N1  - E15. The results show that the optimisation strategy operates effectively under a range of loads and is applicable to different system designs requiring only readily available inputs. Optimal fan speed control results in the minimum daily energy consumption for the bypass fan coil battery in heating mode. The comparison between the estimated fan coil capacity and the measured capacity of the fan coil unit shows a good performance of the correlations for all air flow setting points at higher water flow rates.. 
N1  - E16. The results show that the optimization strategy operates effectively under a range of loads and is applicable to different system designs, and that significant energy savings can be achieved compared to other control strategies.. 
N1  - E17. Conclusions. 
N1  - E18. The study concludes that the developed control strategy is effective in reducing power consumption while maintaining thermal comfort. The strategy has the potential to be adapted to different system designs where fan coil batteries are used as emitters. The use of minimal input data reduces the probability of malfunctioning instrumentation and associated data errors.. 
N1  - E19. The conclusions drawn from the study are that the proposed control strategy is effective in optimizing fan coil battery performance, and that significant energy savings can be achieved compared to other control strategies. 
