TI  - Field test of a ground-source heat pump system and numerical investigation on advanced control strategies
T2  - Applied Thermal Engineering
VL  - 246
PY  - 2024
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2024
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.applthermaleng.2024.123008
AB  - Ground source heat pump (GSHP) systems have been widely studied for space heating in recent years. Aiming at reducing the power consumption of GSHP, this study focuses on leveraging advanced load-predictive control strategies to minimize unnecessary power consumption in circulating pumps under variable load operation. Taking a GSHP system in an office building as a typical case, a field test was conducted and the heating performance in 2023 was obtained. By comparing measured data with the rated performance, the limitations of the existing control strategy were identified. To investigate the energy-saving potential of new control strategies, a transient GSHP simulation model was developed in TRNSYS. Based on the experimentally validated model, two load-predictive control strategies were numerically studied. By modulating the flow rate and supply water temperature, the two advanced control strategies can achieve energy-saving of 9.9% and 11.0%, respectively.
KW  - Heat pumps
KW  - Ground source heat pump
KW  - water pump
KW  - Control strategy
KW  - field test
KW  - TRNSYS
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. For simplicity and reduced cost, many traditional Ground source heat pump (GSHP) systems operate at constant flow rates and constant supply water temperature, which are determined under the rated full load condition [13]. 
N1  - C2. This paper investigated a typical GSHP system by analyzing the field test results and revealing the performance limitations of its current control strategy. 
N1  - C3. The results of this study are summarized as follows: 1. Field test results indicated that the GSHP system operated stably. 
N1  - C4. For the baseline CCS control strategy, due to the constant flow rates, the input power of water pumps was around 10 kW. These results indicated that lowering the supply water temperature caused an increase in the COP from 3.5 to 4.5. 
N1  - C5. 2. A GSHP system simulation model was established in TRNSYS, and its accuracy was validated using the measured data. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. More than 30 % of the global energy consumption is attributed to buildings [3], with heating, ventilation, and air conditioning systems accounting for about 65 % of this energy use in China [4]. 
N1  - D2. Pipeline heat loss accounted for over 50 % of the total heat absorbed from the soil. 
N1  - D3. CCS featured the lowest load-side WP energy consumption at 70.65 kW⋅h, which was half of VCS and 60 % lower than VVS. 
N1  - D4. Meanwhile, the difference in daily energy consumption of the HP and source-side water pump was less than 1 %. 
N1  - D5. Compared to CCS, VCS showed a 5.2 % increase in total daily energy consumption. 
N1  - D6. On the other hand, although VVS had a lower energy consumption in the load-side water pump than VCS, the total daily energy consumption of VVS was also 3.9 % higher than CCS. 
N1  - D7. VCS and VVS reduced daily energy consumption by 18.8 % and 20.4 % compared to CCS, respectively. 
N1  - D8. Compared to the baseline control strategy CCS, VCS achieved a 9.9 % reduction, primarily due to the source-side water pump. 
N1  - D9. Based on this, two load-predictive control strategies were proposed, VCS and VVS, and demonstrated more than 10 % energy saving from the baseline control strategy. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The study investigates the energy-saving potential of advanced control strategies for a ground source heat pump (GSHP) system in an office building, finding that two proposed control strategies, VCS and VVS, can achieve energy savings of 9.9% and 11.0%, respectively, compared to the baseline control strategy.. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The study reveals that the existing control strategy of the GSHP system has limitations, and two advanced control strategies based on load prediction can achieve energy-saving of 9.9% and 11.0%, respectively. The study also highlights the importance of load prediction in achieving quantitative modulation of flow rate and supply water temperature.. 
N1  - E5. The key findings of the study include the development of two new control strategies, VCS (Variable flow rate, Constant temperature Strategy) and VVS (Variable flow rate, Variable temperature Strategy), which can improve the energy efficiency of the GSHP system compared to the traditional CCS (Constant flow rate, Constant temperature Strategy). The study also found that the optimal supply water temperature varies depending on the building load, and that modulating the supply water temperature and flow rate can lead to significant energy savings.. 
N1  - E6. The study found that pipeline heat loss accounted for over 50% of the total heat absorbed from the soil, and that modulating supply water temperature based on load variations can improve system performance and energy efficiency. The study also found that the system operated more efficiently when the load was stable, and that the coefficient of performance (COP) increased when the supply water temperature was lowered.. 
N1  - E7. For the baseline CCS control strategy, due to the constant flow rates, the input power of water pumps was around 10 kW. These results indicated that lowering the supply water temperature caused an increase in the COP from 3.5 to 4.5. 
N1  - E8. 2. A GSHP system simulation model was established in TRNSYS, and its accuracy was validated using the measured data. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The objectives of the study are to analyze the performance of the GSHP system, develop and evaluate new control strategies to improve its energy efficiency, and provide reference data for the GSHP community. The study aims to maximize energy efficiency and reduce operational costs while ensuring user thermal comfort.. 
N1  - E11. Methods. 
N1  - E12. The study conducts a field test of the GSHP system and develops a transient simulation model using TRNSYS. The study also proposes two advanced control strategies based on load prediction, which adjust the flow rates and the supply water temperature of the heat pump.. 
N1  - E13. The methods used in the study include field measurements of the GSHP system's performance, development of simulation models using TRNSYS, and analysis of the measured and simulated data. The study also involves the development of new control strategies, VCS and VVS, and their evaluation using the simulation model.. 
N1  - E14. The study used field test data and simulation models to analyze the performance of the GSHP system. The study also used pressure-enthalpy diagrams to analyze the refrigeration cycle.. 
N1  - E15. The study uses a combination of field testing and numerical simulation to investigate the GSHP system. The simulation model is developed in TRNSYS and validated using measured data. The study also uses numerical investigations to evaluate the energy-saving potential of the proposed control strategies.. 
N1  - E16. Results. 
N1  - E17. The study reveals that the two advanced control strategies can achieve energy-saving of 9.9% and 11.0%, respectively. The study also shows that the existing control strategy of the GSHP system has limitations, and the proposed control strategies can improve the system's energy efficiency.. 
N1  - E18. The results of the study show that the GSHP system's performance can be improved by using the new control strategies, VCS and VVS, which can reduce energy consumption and improve thermal comfort. The study also found that the optimal supply water temperature varies depending on the building load, and that modulating the supply water temperature and flow rate can lead to significant energy savings.. 
N1  - E19. The study found that the GSHP system operated efficiently when the supply water temperature was lowered, and that the COP increased when the load was stable. The study also found that the simulation model had some errors compared to the field test data, particularly in predicting the load and source-side temperatures.. 
N1  - E20. Conclusions. 
N1  - E21. The study concludes that advanced load-predictive control strategies can improve the energy efficiency of GSHP systems. The study also highlights the importance of load prediction in achieving quantitative modulation of flow rate and supply water temperature.. 
N1  - E22. The conclusions of the study are that the new control strategies, VCS and VVS, can improve the energy efficiency of the GSHP system, and that the optimal supply water temperature varies depending on the building load. The study also concludes that modulating the supply water temperature and flow rate can lead to significant energy savings.. 
N1  - E23. The study concluded that modulating supply water temperature based on load variations is necessary for energy conservation, and that pipeline heat loss should be considered in the design and operation of GSHP systems. The study also highlighted the importance of accurate simulation models in predicting the performance of GSHP systems.. 
N1  - E24. The study concludes that the proposed control strategies, VCS and VVS, are effective in achieving energy savings for a GSHP system. The study also highlights the importance of considering the location and load profile when evaluating the energy-saving potential of control strategies. 
