N1  - B. Participants: 3 closely related studies. 
N1  - B1. Optimal sizing based on computational heat transfer approaches. In this category, we will review three closely related studies. The first one is the work of Gultekin et al [31], where a numerical approach to optimize the distance between boreholes for a set of borefield configurations as shown in Fig. 4(a) was considered. 
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. Future worries on finite fossil energy resources and necessity of environmental friendliness have made renewable energy use popular and widespread in recent years. 
N1  - C2. Mathematical model based optimal design or co-design depends on an accurate mathematical model of the underlying borefield subsystem and other subcomponents of a Ground-Coupled Heat Pump (GCHP) or Hybrid Ground-Coupled Heat Pump (HyGCHP) system. 
N1  - C3. The bottleneck here is borefield modelling to predict the thermal behaviour of a general borefield system accounting for the thermal interaction between boreholes, the effect of groundwater flow, the effect of a dynamic ground surface boundary condition, multi-layer ground characteristics, combination of short-term and longterm effects, etc. 
N1  - C4. Thermal response factor models and statespace models can be used for borefield modelling. 
N1  - C5. Thermal response factor models can be used in approaches based on rules of thumb and state-space models can be used in a numerical optimization approach. 
N1  - C6. A nice and interesting exercise would be to compare different categories of optimal design, and if possible co-design, on a benchmark case to have an idea of the comparative performances of the different approaches presented. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. The results are shown in Fig. 15(b) as a comparison to the normal grid with equal distributed BHE loads. They observed that the optimization based on load optimization leads to a relative improvement of 30% compared to the reference case. 
N1  - D2. Mass flow rate (kg/s) load ratio and to the configuration of the borefield. At the end, they claimed that the use of GLHEPro results in an over-sized borefield with a 35% higher total cost. 
N1  - D3. Compared to Yavuzturk and Spitler [30], they achieved an improvement of 17% in life cycle cost, 10% in annual energy usage but 7% increase in total investment cost. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. This paper reviews the literature on optimal design of Ground-Coupled Heat Pump (GCHP) systems, discussing the challenges and approaches to modelling and optimal control, and highlighting the need for intensified research on mathematical models and numerical optimization schemes.. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The key findings of the study include the importance of accurate mathematical modelling of the borefield subsystem and other subcomponents of GCHP systems. The study also highlights the challenges of borefield modelling, including the thermal interaction between boreholes, groundwater flow, and dynamic ground surface boundary conditions.. 
N1  - E5. The bottleneck here is borefield modelling to predict the thermal behaviour of a general borefield system accounting for the thermal interaction between boreholes, the effect of groundwater flow, the effect of a dynamic ground surface boundary condition, multi-layer ground characteristics, combination of short-term and longterm effects, etc. 
N1  - E6. Thermal response factor models and statespace models can be used for borefield modelling. 
N1  - E7. Thermal response factor models can be used in approaches based on rules of thumb and state-space models can be used in a numerical optimization approach. 
N1  - E8. A nice and interesting exercise would be to compare different categories of optimal design, and if possible co-design, on a benchmark case to have an idea of the comparative performances of the different approaches presented. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The objectives of the study are to review the literature on optimal design of GCHP systems, identify the challenges and approaches to modelling and optimal control, and highlight the need for future research. The study aims to provide a comprehensive overview of the current state of knowledge in the field.. 
N1  - E11. Methods. 
N1  - E12. The study uses a literature review approach, examining the existing research on optimal design of GCHP systems. The study also considers the use of mathematical models, numerical optimization schemes, and artificial neural networks (ANNs) for optimal design and control.. 
N1  - E13. Results. 
N1  - E14. The results of the study show that the co-design approach is the best strategy for achieving optimal global system performance. The study also finds that rule/chart-based optimal design is currently the dominant approach, but intensified research on mathematical models and numerical optimization schemes is expected in the near future.. 
N1  - E15. Conclusions. 
N1  - E16. The conclusions of the study highlight the importance of accurate mathematical modelling of GCHP systems and the need for future research on optimal design and control. The study also emphasizes the challenges of borefield modelling and the need for generic approaches that can be applied to complex borefield systems. 
