AB  - In this paper, a compact overview of the state-of-the-art in modeling of ground-coupled heat pump (GCHP) systems and an in-depth review of their optimal control along with the associated research challenges are given. The main focus is on optimal control but since design of an optimal controller may require a model, a relatively short literature review of modeling approaches is also discussed. Adopting the adage “a picture is worth a thousand words”, we tried to include a minimal number of representative schematics and result figures for some of the reviewed studies for clarity and a better understanding of the presented material. In addition to the literature review, we included our comments, points of view, alternative solutions and some potential future directions. This review paper is useful both for engineers and researchers involved in modeling and optimal control of GCHP systems. The second part of the paper, “Ground-Coupled Heat Pumps: Part 2 – Literature Review and Research Challenges in Optimal Design”, focuses on the literature review on optimal design and the associated design challenges for GCHP systems.
KW  - Optimal control
KW  - Artificial Neural Networks
KW  - state space model
KW  - dynamics
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. Despite the low public awareness of this energy-efficient technology based on geothermal energy sources, ground-coupled heat pump (GCHP) have been in commercial use for more than 50 years. 
N1  - C2. Atam and Helsen [57] presented a convex approach through convexification for non-convex optimal control-based and nonconvex model predictive control-based energy-use minimization of buildings equipped with hybrid ground-coupled heat pump systems. 
N1  - C3. Mathematical model-based optimal control depends on an accurate mathematical control model of the underlying borefield subsystem and other subcomponents of a GCHP or hybrid ground-coupled heat pump (HyGCHP) system. 
N1  - C4. The bottleneck here is control-oriented borefield modeling to predict the thermal behavior of a general borefield system accounting for the thermal interaction between boreholes, the effect of groundwater flow, the effect of a dynamic ground surface boundary condition, multi-layer ground characteristics, combination of short-term and long-term effects, etc. 
N1  - C5. Thermal response factor models and statespace models can be used for borefield modeling. 
N1  - C6. The energy saving performance of MPC was 1–3% better compared to the conventional controller, which increased to 7.5% when night setback was considered. 
N1  - C7. In general, Artificial Neural Networks (ANNs) models are not appropriate to design a controller to work in a long time period of 15–20 years (typical for (Hy)GCHPs). 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. In Fig. 5, the ESC controller is tuned at t 1⁄4 50; 000 s and the ESC controller achieves the estimated optima in the static maps, with steady-state errors less than 3% in both cases. 
N1  - D2. The results showed that the performance of MPC and linear optimal controllers were close to dynamic programming-based control results: less than 10% performance deterioration. 
N1  - D3. Without night setback, the energy saving performance of MPC was 1–3% better compared to the conventional controller, which increased to 7.5% when night setback was considered. 
N1  - D4. On the other hand, if the fixed capacity GCHP is designed to cover more than 65% of peak building load, then SPFs are close to each other. 
N1  - D5. The fixed capacity GCHP systems have a higher SPF when the auxiliary heater is designed to cover less than 5% of the annual heat demand. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The optimal control of hybrid ground-coupled heat pump (HyGCHP) systems is a complex task that requires accurate mathematical models and advanced control strategies, with various approaches having been proposed in the literature, including model predictive control, extremum seeking control, and convex optimization.. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The key findings of the study include the importance of accurate modeling of GCHP systems for optimal control, the challenges in obtaining an accurate model of a borefield, and the need for intelligent control strategies to determine quantitative values of optimal load sharing between components. The study also highlights the usefulness of mathematical model-based control approaches, particularly those using linear-time invariant state-space models.. 
N1  - E5. The bottleneck here is control-oriented borefield modeling to predict the thermal behavior of a general borefield system accounting for the thermal interaction between boreholes, the effect of groundwater flow, the effect of a dynamic ground surface boundary condition, multi-layer ground characteristics, combination of short-term and long-term effects, etc. 
N1  - E6. Thermal response factor models and statespace models can be used for borefield modeling. 
N1  - E7. The energy saving performance of MPC was 1–3% better compared to the conventional controller, which increased to 7.5% when night setback was considered. 
N1  - E8. In general, Artificial Neural Networks (ANNs) models are not appropriate to design a controller to work in a long time period of 15–20 years (typical for (Hy)GCHPs). 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The objectives of the study are to provide a comprehensive review of the state-of-the-art in modeling and optimal control of GCHP systems, to highlight the challenges and opportunities in this field, and to suggest future directions for research. The study aims to provide a useful resource for engineers and researchers involved in modeling and optimal control of GCHP systems.. 
N1  - E11. Methods. 
N1  - E12. The methods used in the study include a literature review of modeling approaches for GCHP systems, a review of optimal control strategies for GCHP systems, and an analysis of the challenges and opportunities in this field. The study also uses numerical approaches, such as dynamic programming, to evaluate the performance of different control strategies.. 
N1  - E13. Results. 
N1  - E14. The results of the study show that mathematical model-based control approaches are the most useful and preferable ones for optimal control of GCHP systems. The study also shows that the performance of model predictive control (MPC) and linear optimal controllers are close to dynamic programming-based control results, with less than 10% performance deterioration.. 
N1  - E15. Conclusions. 
N1  - E16. The conclusions of the study are that accurate modeling of GCHP systems is essential for optimal control, and that mathematical model-based control approaches are the most useful and preferable ones for optimal control of GCHP systems. The study also concludes that there are still challenges and opportunities in this field, and that further research is needed to develop more accurate models and control strategies.. 
N1  - E17. The conclusions of the study highlight the importance of accurate mathematical models and advanced control strategies for optimal control of HyGCHP systems, and identify the challenges and limitations of current control methods, including the need for more accurate borefield modeling and the development of generic approaches. 
