N1  - D8. It was shown that there was no significant difference if the on/off controlled GSHP can cover more than 65% of the building peak heating demand. 
N1  - D9. Compared to the original design, the entropy generation rate decreased by 25.6% while the integrated evaluation factor increased by 15.3%. 
N1  - D10. The use of GRG2 as the optimization tool provided a more stable optimal solution and the new method can save the total annual cost of more than 10%. 
N1  - D11. A reduction in annual power consumption of 28% was achieved, compared to the nominal frequency operation. 
N1  - D12. The strategy can result in a reduction in cooling power consumption of 4.2%, as compared to a two-stage control strategy. 
N1  - D13. The strategy can result in a reduction in cooling power consumption of 4.2%, as compared to a two-stage control strategy. It can reduce the cooling and heating power consumption of 9.0% and 8.0% respectively, as compared to a rule-based strategy. This strategy can reduce the total system energy consumption of 4.15%, as compared to a baseline strategy. 
N1  - D14. It can reduce the cooling and heating power consumption of 9.0% and 8.0% respectively, as compared to a rule-based strategy. This strategy can reduce the total system energy consumption of 4.15%, as compared to a baseline strategy. This strategy can save up to 18% energy as compared to the conventional control strategies. 
N1  - D15. An increase in the seasonal system performance of over 20% in heating and 40% in cooling can be achieved, as compared to the nominal frequency operation. 
N1  - D16. The new strategy can increase the seasonal performance factor of 33%, as compared to another control strategy. 
N1  - D17. This strategy can save 9.3% energy consumption, as compared to that using constant settings. This strategy can save 18.3% energy in comparison to an on/off control strategy. This strategy can save 7.8% and 7.1% energy and generate 4.4% and 6.2% more electricity in the cooling and heating operations, compared to a baseline strategy. 
N1  - D18. The strategy can reduce the operational cost by 12.56% based on seven days test, compared to the empirical operation. 
N1  - D19. This strategy can reduce the operational cost by 7.8% in the coldest month, compared to a conventional set-point controller. 
N1  - D20. The strategy can reduce gas consumption by 23%, compared to the existing control strategy. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. This paper provides an overview of recent advances and developments in optimal design and control of Ground Source Heat Pump (GSHP) systems, highlighting the need for optimization to enhance energy efficiency and reduce payback periods.. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The results show that single-objective and multi-objective design optimization strategies for GSHP systems have been developed, which are more robust than commonly used rule-based design approaches, and that optimal control can provide better operating performance of GSHP systems.. 
N1  - E5. The results demonstrated the importance of varying water flow rates in GSHP systems for improved energy efficiency. 
N1  - E6. The results showed that the nonlinear model predictive control (MPC) exhibited a better performance than the other two strategies when all the factors such as applicability, and savings in annual cost were taken into account simutaneously. 
N1  - E7. To solve soil temperature fluctuation, performance analysis for the first 10 years is important during the design. 17% decrease in total present value cost, 10% decrease in annual energy use, and a 7% increase in total initial cost as compared with the results from another study. 
N1  - E8. This work discussed the general issues related to the optimization of GSHP systems and provided an overview of recent progress and. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The main objectives of the study are to define appropriate optimization objectives, identify key variables to be optimized, formulate the optimization problem, and solve the optimization problem using an appropriate method.. 
N1  - E11. Methods. 
N1  - E12. The study uses a model-based approach to formulate the control problem, and model predictive control is identified as a key method for optimizing GSHP systems.. 
N1  - E13. Results. 
N1  - E14. The results demonstrate the importance of varying water flow rates in GSHP systems for improved energy efficiency, and that nonlinear model predictive control exhibits better performance than other strategies.. 
N1  - E15. Conclusions. 
N1  - E16. The study concludes that optimal design and control of GSHP systems are essential to enhance energy efficiency and reduce payback periods, and that further research is needed to develop energy-efficient control strategies and evaluate their control reliability, effectiveness, and long-term performance. 
