TI  - An optimal control method for small-scale GSHP-integrated air-conditioning system to improve indoor thermal environment control
T2  - Journal of Building Engineering
VL  - 59
PY  - 2022
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2022
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.jobe.2022.105140
AB  - ON/OFF controlled ground-source heat pump (GSHP) is often integrated with small-scale central air-conditioning system (ACS) for villas. Chilled-water temperature (CHWT) in this system usually oscillates in a large range due to the ON/OFF switch of the compressor. As it may be too high or too low, the cooling output of the air-handling unit (AHU) in its adjustable range may not be coincident with the indoor cooling demand, which may lead to unsatisfactory room temperature control performance. This study proposes an optimal control method for this system to improve the control robustness of the room temperature. Usually, the variation of indoor cooling-load is transferred to the chilled-water resulting in changing water temperature. In this method, the optimal chilled-water return (CHWR) temperature is calculated on the basis of the estimated sensible heat load and total cooling-load of the concerned rooms. Then, this CHWR temperature is reset following up the ON and OFF states of the compressor to regulate the variation range of the CHWT. Thus, the cooling output of the AHU in its adjustable range can always follow up the changing of indoor cooling demand. This method is tested on a simulation platform and a real ACS. The test results show good temperature control performance can be achieved. The experimental results demonstrate that the maximum deviation of room temperature is reduced by 2◦C and the energy consumption of the GSHP and fan can be reduced by 30.12% at light cooling-load. The application performance shows this method can be easily realized in a common building automation system.
KW  - ground-source heat pump
KW  - Optimal control
KW  - air-conditioning system
KW  - room temperature
KW  - temperature control
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. Small-scale ground-source heat pump (i.e., GSHP) integrated central air-conditioning systems (i.e., ACSs) are well suited and widely used in the small-scale and low-density residential buildings, such as villas, rural houses, or some small offices [1,2,3]. 
N1  - C2. This study presents an optimal control method for an ON/OFF controlled GSHP-integrated central air-conditioning system (i.e., ACS) so as to improve the control performance of the indoor thermal environment. 
N1  - C3. The chilled-water return temperature (i.e., CHWR) is optimized based on estimated cooling-load. It can regulate the cooling output capacity of the air-handling unit (AHU) following up the ON and OFF states of the GSHP to always satisfy the changing of the indoor cooling demand. 
N1  - C4. The control performance of this optimal control method was tested in the simulation platform of a GSHP-integrated central ACS. 
N1  - C5. The test results show that a better control robustness and accuracy of room temperature can be achieved by using the optimal control method in a typical summer day as well as in a typical spring day. 
N1  - C6. The results show that the system energy use can be reduced significantly at light load condition by using the optimal control method. In this test, the energy consumption was reduced by 30.12% in total of GSHP and fan. 
N1  - C7. The experiment results show that the optimal control method can obtain much better room temperature control performance when comparing with the basic control method. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. The experimental results demonstrate that the maximum deviation of room temperature is reduced by 2◦C and the energy consumption of the GSHP and fan can be reduced by 30.12% at light cooling-load. 
N1  - D2. The total energy consumption of GSHP and fan can be reduced by 7.63% in the spring day. 
N1  - D3. The results also show that the system energy use can be reduced significantly at light load condition by using the optimal control method. In this test, the energy consumption was reduced by 30.12% in total of GSHP and fan. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. This study proposes an optimal control method for a small-scale ground-source heat pump (GSHP) integrated central air-conditioning system (ACS) to improve the control robustness of the room temperature, which can reduce the maximum deviation of room temperature by 2°C and the energy consumption of the GSHP and fan by 30.12% at light cooling-load.. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The key findings of the study include the development of an optimal control method that can calculate the optimal chilled-water return (CHWR) temperature set-point online using an AHU model according to the estimated real-time sensible heat load and total cooling-load of the concerned rooms. The study also found that the proposed control method can reduce the maximum deviation of room temperature by 2°C and the energy consumption of the GSHP and fan by 30.12% at light cooling-load.. 
N1  - E5. The optimal control algorithm can improve the control robustness of room temperature under different cooling-load conditions by adjusting the set-point of the chilled water return (CHWR) temperature. The algorithm uses the average sensible heat load and total cooling-load to assess the cooling-load condition and adjust the CHWR temperature set-point accordingly.. 
N1  - E6. The key findings of the study include: (1) the optimal control method can achieve better control robustness and accuracy of room temperature, (2) the total energy consumption of GSHP and fan can be reduced by 7.63% in the spring day, and (3) the system energy use can be reduced significantly at light load condition by using the optimal control method, with a reduction of 30.12% in total of GSHP and fan.. 
N1  - E7. The experiment results show that the optimal control method can obtain much better room temperature control performance when comparing with the basic control method. 
N1  - E8. Objectives. 
N1  - E9. The objective of the study is to improve the control robustness of the room temperature in a small-scale GSHP-integrated central ACS by developing an advanced optimal control method. The study aims to address the issue of unsatisfactory room temperature control performance in GSHP-integrated central ACSs.. 
N1  - E10. Methods. 
N1  - E11. The study proposes an optimal control method that calculates the optimal CHWR temperature set-point online using an AHU model according to the estimated real-time sensible heat load and total cooling-load of the concerned rooms. The method is tested on a simulation platform and a real ACS.. 
N1  - E12. The study uses a simulation platform developed by the Transient Simulation Program (TRNSYS) to validate the control performance of the optimal control method. The simulation platform includes a GSHP, a GSHE, a water pump, a water tank, and an AHU, and is used to test the optimal control algorithm under different cooling-load conditions. The algorithm uses the average sensible heat load and total cooling-load to assess the cooling-load condition and adjust the CHWR temperature set-point accordingly.. 
N1  - E13. The study uses a simulation platform and an experimental test to evaluate the performance of the optimal control method, which involves regulating the CHWR temperature set-point based on estimated cooling-load.. 
N1  - E14. Results. 
N1  - E15. The results of the study show that the proposed control method can achieve good temperature control performance. The experimental results demonstrate that the maximum deviation of room temperature is reduced by 2°C and the energy consumption of the GSHP and fan can be reduced by 30.12% at light cooling-load.. 
N1  - E16. The simulation results show that the optimal control algorithm can improve the control robustness of room temperature under different cooling-load conditions. The room temperature was tracked robustly and accurately, with a mean absolute tracking error of 0.07°C and a maximum deviation of 0.4°C at steady state phase. The CHWR temperature set-point was adjusted to change the variation range of the CHWR temperature for meeting the heavy cooling-load.. 
N1  - E17. Conclusions. 
N1  - E18. The study concludes that the proposed optimal control method can improve the control robustness of the room temperature in a small-scale GSHP-integrated central ACS. The method can reduce the maximum deviation of room temperature and energy consumption of the GSHP and fan.. 
N1  - E19. The study concludes that the optimal control algorithm can improve the control robustness of room temperature under different cooling-load conditions by adjusting the set-point of the CHWR temperature. The algorithm is effective in tracking the room temperature robustly and accurately, and can be used to improve the energy efficiency of GSHP-integrated ACS. 
