N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The paper reviews the current state and potential future developments of ground-source heat pump (GSHP) technology, focusing on its application in refurbishing existing buildings in the European Union, where 40% of final energy consumption is attributed to the buildings sector.. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The minimum Rb values achievable with present-day best solutions in terms of pipe and grout materials range from 0.06 to 0.08 K/(m·W), and the use of nanomaterials can increase thermal conductivity, while the choice of grout material's thermal conductivity and heat capacity is crucial for the efficiency of the GSHP.. 
N1  - E5. Key findings include the importance of considering the interaction between the borefield and ground in GSHP system design, and the need for optimal control strategies to minimize energy consumption and maximize comfort.. 
N1  - E6. The key findings of the study include the definition of optimal values for thermal conductivity of pipes and grout materials in GSHPs, the potential of new materials such as thermally enhanced HDPE and graphite thermally enhanced grouts, and the importance of proper design and control of GSHP plants.. 
N1  - E7. The low thermal conductivity of Phase change materials (PCM) has necessitated the introduction of thermally enhanced PCMs, whose performances, in terms of thermal conductivity, are still not comparable to those of controlled lowstrength materials (CLSM) and conventional spiked grouts. 
