N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The objectives of the study include evaluating the role of penalty temperature in GSHP system design, comparing different design and control optimization methods, and identifying the most effective approaches for optimal GSHP system operation.. 
N1  - E11. Methods. 
N1  - E12. The study reviews the state of the art of borehole heat exchangers, focusing on parameters such as pipe and grout materials, and provides an overview of recent developments involving refrigerants and secondary fluids.. 
N1  - E13. The methods used in the study include mathematical model-based methods, rule-based controls, and model predictive controls, as well as a review of existing literature on GSHP system design and control optimization.. 
N1  - E14. Results. 
N1  - E15. The results show that the use of enhanced HDPE and coaxial pipe configuration can reduce the required borehole length up to 23%, and the combination of phase change materials (PCMs) and nanomaterials can improve the thermal conductivity of the grout.. 
N1  - E16. The results of the study show that model-based optimal control strategies can achieve significant energy savings and improve GSHP system performance, and that the choice of a co-design approach can allow for integrated optimal design and control.. 
N1  - E17. The results of the study include the identification of optimal values for thermal conductivity of pipes and grout materials, and the potential of new materials and designs.. 
N1  - E18. Conclusions. 
N1  - E19. The study concludes that GSHP technology has the potential to be a cost-effective solution for reducing energy consumption in the buildings sector, but its spread is hindered by obstacles such as high installation costs and lack of awareness among stakeholders.. 
N1  - E20. The conclusions of the study highlight the importance of optimal design and control for GSHP systems, and the need for further research on the development of effective optimization methods.. 
N1  - E21. The conclusions of the study are that GSHPs have a great potential for reducing energy demand, but their use is limited by economic issues and low awareness, and that proper design and control of GSHP plants are crucial for their efficient operation. 
