TI  - Control of Heat Transfer in a Vertical Ground Heat Exchanger for a Geothermal Heat Pump System
T2  - Energies
VL  - 15
PY  - 2022
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2022
UR  - 
DO  - 10.3390/en15145300
AB  - This paper presents a mathematical model of heat transfer behavior between the liquid inside vertical underground geothermal pipes and the surrounding ground for heating (in the winter) and cooling (in the summer) modes in a ground heat exchanger (GHE) that can optimize its output temperature. The GHE’s output temperature reaches the appropriate value when the water velocity is lowered enough. Subsequently, the proposed model was applied to a case study of a 400-ton geothermal heat pump system (GHPS) at Oakland University, in both the heating and cooling modes, to assess its validity and improve the GHE’s performance. The model was implemented in MATLAB using an ordinary differential equation (ODE) solver. Four different water velocities were used to demonstrate the significant effect of velocity on the loop exit temperature. Model predictive control (MPC) was designed to optimize the GHE’s output temperature by controlling the water velocity, which could reduce the energy consumption used for heat and water circulating pumps. The results reveal that the acceptable range of the water velocity for Oakland University’s GHE was between 0.35 and 0.45 m/s, which ensured that the heat pump system delivered the proper temperature to provide the Human Health Building (HHB) with a comfortable temperature regardless of the season. The suggested water velocity ranges in vertical single U-tube pipes with diameters of De 25 mm, De 32 mm, and De 40 mm are between 0.33 and 0.43 m/s, 0.35 to 0.45 m/s, and 0.38 to 0.48 m/s, respectively.
KW  - ground heat exchanger
KW  - heat transfer
KW  - Heat Pump
KW  - model predictive control
KW  - high-density polyethylene
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. To take advantage of the existing heat contained within the earth, an efficient technology known as the geothermal heat pump system (GHPS) was proposed. 
N1  - C2. The proposed model was applied to a case study, Oakland University’s GHPS, to evaluate its validation and enhance the ground heat exchanger (GHE)’s performance. 
N1  - C3. The output temperature only reached the ground for both modes when the water velocity was between 0.35 m/s and 0.45 m/s. 
N1  - C4. The proposed model was validated when the GHE’s output temperature reached that of the ground. 
N1  - C5. We identified that the optimum ranges of water velocity in vertical single U-tube pipes with diameters of De 25 mm, De 32 mm, and. 
N1  - C6. The heating and cooling efficiencies of the GHPS outperform conventional heating and air conditioning systems, which are 30% to 70% higher than conventional heating, and 20% to 50% higher than air conditioning systems. 
N1  - C7. We concluded that the designed linear model predictive control (MPC) can control nonlinear heat transfer in a vertical ground heat exchanger system around the desired equilibrium point. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. The heating and cooling efficiencies of the GHPS outperform conventional heating and air conditioning systems, which are 30% to 70% higher than conventional heating, and 20% to 50% higher than air conditioning systems. 
N1  - D2. Furthermore, the GHPS significantly reduces electricity use by approximately 75% in compassion to conventional heating or cooling systems [1]. 
N1  - D3. Therefore, the GHPS is considered a clean, reliable, renewable, and sustainable source of energy that requires very low maintenance and is unaffected by climatic conditions. It is also environmentally friendly and greatly reduces CO2 emissions by up to 52% when compared to heating, ventilation, and air conditioning (HVAC) [1,2,3,4,5,6]. 
N1  - D4. Its initial capital cost is significant, roughly 30–50% higher than traditional heating and air conditioning systems [9]. 
N1  - D5. Dehkordi and Schincariol [20] demonstrated that increasing the thermal conductivity of backfill material has a positive impact on heat extraction. They pointed out that by using the high thermal conductivity of the backfill material (3 W/m·K), heat extraction can be increased by more than 10% in comparison to the low thermal conductivity of the backfill material (1 W/m·K). 
N1  - D6. For instance, Bassiouny et al [26] mixed miniature aluminum wires with a conventional HDPE pipe material. They found that when an additional 2 mm of aluminum wire was added to the material of the pipe itself, the thermal conductivity rose 25%; when an additional 3 mm of aluminum wire was added to the material of the pipe itself, the thermal conductivity rose 150%, when compared to the original HDPE. 
N1  - D7. The result demonstrated that the enhanced pipe material’s thermal conductivity increased by 75%, from 0.4 W/m·K to 0.7 W/m·K, as compared to the original HDPE pipe. 
N1  - D8. The results also showed that this improvement reduced the thermal resistance of the borehole by 24%. 
N1  - D9. The new design pipe was able to increase heat extraction by approximately 7% in comparison to the original pipe. 
N1  - D10. If the proportion is reduced to 20%, it could enhance the working fluid’s physical properties, maximizing the heat transfer between water and its surrounding grounds [39]. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. A mathematical model of heat transfer behavior in a ground heat exchanger (GHE) is proposed to optimize its output temperature by controlling the water velocity, which can reduce energy consumption.. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The acceptable range of water velocity for Oakland University’s GHE is between 0.35 and 0.45 m/s, and the optimum ranges of water velocity in vertical single U-tube pipes with diameters of De 25 mm, De 32 mm, and De 40 mm are between 0.33 and 0.43 m/s, 0.35 to 0.45 m/s, and 0.38 to 0.48 m/s, respectively.. 
N1  - E5. We identified that the optimum ranges of water velocity in vertical single U-tube pipes with diameters of De 25 mm, De 32 mm, and. 
N1  - E6. The heating and cooling efficiencies of the GHPS outperform conventional heating and air conditioning systems, which are 30% to 70% higher than conventional heating, and 20% to 50% higher than air conditioning systems. 
N1  - E7. We concluded that the designed linear model predictive control (MPC) can control nonlinear heat transfer in a vertical ground heat exchanger system around the desired equilibrium point. 
N1  - E8. Objectives. 
N1  - E9. The objective of the study is to propose a mathematical model of heat transfer behavior in a GHE to optimize its output temperature by controlling the water velocity, and to validate the model using a case study of a GHPS at Oakland University.. 
N1  - E10. Methods. 
N1  - E11. The model was implemented in MATLAB using an ordinary differential equation (ODE) solver, and four different water velocities were used to demonstrate the significant effect of velocity on the loop exit temperature.. 
N1  - E12. The methods used in the research are not described in detail, but it appears that a mathematical model is being developed using various equations and parameters.. 
N1  - E13. Results. 
N1  - E14. The results show that the GHE’s output temperature matches the ground temperature when the water velocity is between 0.35 and 0.45 m/s, and the designed linear model predictive control (MPC) can control nonlinear heat transfer in a vertical ground heat exchanger system around the desired equilibrium point.. 
N1  - E15. Conclusions. 
N1  - E16. The study concludes that the designed linear MPC can control nonlinear heat transfer in a vertical ground heat exchanger system around the desired equilibrium point, and the proposed model can be used to optimize the GHE’s output temperature by controlling the water velocity.. 
N1  - E17. No conclusions are drawn in the text, as it appears to be a preliminary or introductory section. 
