AB  - Deep geothermal energy is a sustainable and renewable spacing heating source. Although many studies have discussed the design optimization of deep borehole systems, few have accomplished optimization and in-depth analysis of system operation control. In this study, an analytical model of the U-type deep borehole heat exchanger is proposed, and the average relative error between the simulated outlet temperatures and experimental data is −3.2%. Then, this paper presents an integrated model for the operation optimization study of the U-type deep-borehole ground source heat pump system. The optimal control of flow rate is adopted to match the variation in heating load. Compared with the constant-flow rate (110 m3/h) operation mode, the variable flow rate method reduces the power consumption of the heat pump and circulating pump by 22.1%, from 288,423 kW·h to 224,592 kW·h, during 2112 h of operation. In addition, the system has a larger RHS and COP when the thermal conductivity of the backfill material increases. When the borehole depth increases by 200 m from 2300 m, the energy consumption of the circulating pump will drop from 85,844 kW·h to 56,548 kW·h. The COP of the heat pump unit will decrease approximately linearly as the heating load increases, and the total power consumption will increase accordingly. This work can provide guidance for the design and optimization of U-shaped GSHP systems.
KW  - coefficient of performance
KW  - ground source heat pump
KW  - flow rate
KW  - geothermal energy
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. Geothermal energy is an important renewable energy source for power generation [1], spacing cooling/heating [2] and some additional preheating processes [3]. 
N1  - C2. In scenarios where the thermal load of buildings significantly exceeds the cooling load, shallow ground source heat pump (GSHP) systems often struggle to meet long-term heating demands, and prolonged heat extraction leads to lowered soil temperatures, resulting in decreased operational efficiency [7]. 
N1  - C3. In the design process of ground source heat pump systems with buried pipes, the drilling depth is typically calculated under the premise of meeting the most unfavorable conditions. 
N1  - C4. This model comprehensively considers the dynamic heat load of terminal buildings and the dynamic heat exchange process on the ground source side. It adopts a variable-flow method to match the dynamic changes in building heat load, while reducing the energy consumption of the ground source loop pump. This enables the calculation of water flow based on known building heat load, achieving optimization of ground source heat pump system operation. 
N1  - C5. Circulating pump power consumption from 117,332 kW·h to 56,548 kW·h, resulting in a total reduction inpower powerconsumption consumptionfrom of 22.1%. 
N1  - C6. If the flow rate is greater than the maximum value, it is necessary to further reduce the outlet temperature of the heat pump, i.e., the inlet temperature of the BHE at7 the of 15next moment. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. In terms of the long-term operation and thermal recovery performance for U-type BHE, the results show that the thermal recovery rate of deep rock and soil is over 91.2% after an eight-month thermal recovery period [18]. 
N1  - D2. Compared with this, variable-flow operation reduces the power consumption of the heat pump unit from 171,090 kW·h to 168,043 kW·h, and the circulating pump power consumption from 117,332 kW·h to 56,548 kW·h, resulting in a total reduction in power consumption of 22.1%. 
N1  - D3. Circulating pump power consumption from 117,332 kW·h to 56,548 kW·h, resulting in a total reduction inpower powerconsumption consumptionfrom of 22.1%. 
N1  - D4. 98.8%, and the heat pump unit m, the average RHS increases from 95.7% to 98.8%, and the heat pump unit COP increases increases from. 
N1  - D5. 20%, during thethe simulation peperiod, the average heat extraction capacity of the ground loop heat exchanger decreases riod, the average heat extraction capacity of the ground loop heat exchanger decreases riod, the average heat extraction capacity of the ground loop heat exchanger decreases from 320 kW to 265 kW, a reduction of 17.2%. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The study proposes a variable-flow operation model for medium-depth U-type ground source heat pump systems, which comprehensively considers the dynamic heat load of terminal buildings and the dynamic heat exchange process on the ground source side, and adopts a variable-flow method to match the dynamic changes in building heat load, reducing energy consumption.. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The study found that the variable flow rate method reduces power consumption by 22.1% compared to the constant-flow rate operation mode, and that the system's energy consumption and COP are affected by parameters such as backfill material, borehole length, and heating load.. 
N1  - E5. The study finds that the variable-flow operation model can reduce the power consumption of the heat pump unit and the circulating pump, resulting in a total reduction in power consumption of 22.1%. The model also prioritizes controlling fluid flow, and when the building heat load decreases, the fluid flow also decreases, reducing the system's heating capacity and avoiding energy waste.. 
N1  - E6. The key findings include that increasing the drilling depth reduces the required flow rate, which significantly reduces the energy consumption of the circulating pump, and that operating the system with variable fluid flow can reduce energy consumption. Additionally, the study found that an increase in the building thermal load leads to a nearly linear decrease in the COP of the heat pump unit and an increase in total power consumption.. 
N1  - E7. Circulating pump power consumption from 117,332 kW·h to 56,548 kW·h, resulting in a total reduction inpower powerconsumption consumptionfrom of 22.1%. 
N1  - E8. If the flow rate is greater than the maximum value, it is necessary to further reduce the outlet temperature of the heat pump, i.e., the inlet temperature of the BHE at7 the of 15next moment. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The objective of the study is to propose a variable-flow operation model for medium-depth U-type ground source heat pump systems, which can optimize the system's performance and reduce energy consumption. The study aims to evaluate the system's performance based on factors such as thermal conductivity of the backfill material, drilling structure, and flow rate of the circulating fluid.. 
N1  - E11. Methods. 
N1  - E12. The study uses a combination of analytical and numerical methods to model the heat transfer process in the U-type deep borehole heat exchanger and to optimize the system's operation.. 
N1  - E13. The study uses simulation calculations to evaluate the performance of the variable-flow operation model, based on the heat load of an office building from 1 December of one year to 28 February of the next year. The study also uses equations to calculate the energy consumption of the heat pump unit and the circulating water pump, as well as the coefficient of performance (COP) of the heat pump unit.. 
N1  - E14. The methods used in the study include establishing an analytical solution heat transfer model for medium-depth U-type ground heat exchangers and simulating the system's performance under different conditions. The study also uses a variable-flow operation model to comprehensively consider the dynamic heating load and heat transfer process of BHEs.. 
N1  - E15. Results. 
N1  - E16. The results show that the variable flow rate method can reduce power consumption and improve energy efficiency, and that the system's performance is affected by various parameters such as backfill material, borehole length, and heating load.. 
N1  - E17. The study finds that the variable-flow operation model can reduce the power consumption of the heat pump unit and the circulating pump, resulting in a total reduction in power consumption of 22.1%. The average COP of the heat pump unit is 5.14, which is higher than the value of 4.99 with constant flow rate. The study also finds that the thermal conductivity of the backfill material has a significant impact on the system's performance, with higher thermal conductivity resulting in higher COP and heating guarantee rate.. 
N1  - E18. The results show that increasing the drilling depth from 2300 m to 2500 m decreases the power consumption of the circulating pump from 85,844 kW·h to 56,548 kW·h. The study also found that operating the system with variable fluid flow can reduce energy consumption, with the average COP of the heat pump increasing from 4.99 to 5.12.. 
N1  - E19. Conclusions. 
N1  - E20. The study concludes that the proposed analytical model and operation optimization model can provide guidance for the design and optimization of U-shaped ground source heat pump systems.. 
N1  - E21. The study concludes that the variable-flow operation model is an effective way to optimize the performance of medium-depth U-type ground source heat pump systems, reducing energy consumption and improving the system's efficiency. The study also concludes that the thermal conductivity of the backfill material has a significant impact on the system's performance, and should be carefully considered in the design and operation of the system.. 
N1  - E22. The conclusions drawn from the study are that increasing the drilling depth and operating the system with variable fluid flow can improve the system's performance and reduce energy consumption. The study also found that the thermal conductivity of the backfill materials and the building thermal load have a significant impact on the system's performance. 
