N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The study analyzes the sizing of a buffer tank for a heat pump system and proposes a design guideline, as well as an optimized control strategy to improve energy performance and user comfort, with results showing the effectiveness of the approach in both cooling and heating modes.. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The key findings of this study include the development of an integrated optimization strategy that achieves significant energy savings, up to 35% in the summer season and 53% in the winter season, while maintaining user comfort. The study also shows that the optimized control strategy outperforms the standard control in terms of energy efficiency.. 
N1  - E5. The study finds that the most restrictive criterion for the maximum system volume is the minimum OFF time, and that a possible solution for tank sizing is to consider a rule of thumb based on the unitary capacity expressed in kW, with a minimum volume provided by the worst-case compressor time. The study also finds that the optimized control strategy is effective in improving energy performance and user comfort in both cooling and heating modes.. 
N1  - E6. The key findings of the study include the development of an energy optimization algorithm that achieves a 35% improvement in the cooling seasonal performance factor and a 53% improvement in the heating seasonal performance factor, while keeping user comfort.. 
N1  - E7. Experimental results for a one-year operation period demonstrate important energy savings, up to 35% in the summer season and 53% in the winter season, while keeping the user comfort. 
N1  - E8. The standard control considered in the rst approach was more restrictive, due to the requirements of GROUND-MED project, whereas the one considered when the upgraded algorithm was tested was a more realistic standard control, considering the minimum standard supply temperature recommended by the heat pump manufacturer. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The objectives of this PhD dissertation are to develop an optimal control strategy for the GSHP system, to minimize energy consumption while maintaining user comfort, and to evaluate the performance of the system under different operating conditions. The study also aims to develop an experimental in-situ optimization methodology for the water circulation pumps frequency.. 
N1  - E11. Methods. 
N1  - E12. The methods used in this study include the development of an integrated optimization strategy, which involves the management of air flow in the fan coil, water mass flow in the internal and external hydraulic systems, and setpoint temperature in the heat pump. The study also employs an experimental in-situ optimization methodology for the water circulation pumps frequency.. 
N1  - E13. The study uses analytical expressions to develop a design guideline for the sizing of buffer tanks, and experiments to evaluate the effectiveness of the optimized control strategy. The study also uses data from a geothermal plant to test the design guideline and the optimized control strategy.. 
N1  - E14. The methods used in the study include the development of an energy optimization algorithm, the analysis of the performance of the GSHP system, and the evaluation of the impact of the algorithm on user comfort and energy savings.. 
N1  - E15. The methods used in the study involved developing and implementing a new optimized control algorithm for the geothermal installation, which was then tested during a cooling season and a heating season.. 
N1  - E16. Results. 
N1  - E17. The results of this study show that the optimized control strategy achieves significant energy savings, up to 35% in the summer season and 53% in the winter season, while maintaining user comfort. The study also shows that the optimized control strategy outperforms the standard control in terms of energy efficiency.. 
N1  - E18. The study finds that the optimized control strategy is effective in improving energy performance and user comfort in both cooling and heating modes. The study also finds that the design guideline provides a minimum volume for the buffer tank that is sufficient for the heat pump system. The experimental results show that the actual tank temperature tracks the calculated building supply temperature, and that the optimized control strategy saves energy by adjusting the temperature setpoint and the frequency of the water circulation pumps.. 
N1  - E19. The results of the study show that the energy optimization algorithm achieves a 35% improvement in the cooling seasonal performance factor and a 53% improvement in the heating seasonal performance factor, while keeping user comfort.. 
N1  - E20. The results of the study showed a significant improvement in energy performance, with a 35% increase in the seasonal performance factor in cooling mode and a 53% increase in heating mode, as well as positive feedback from users regarding comfort.. 
N1  - E21. Conclusions. 
N1  - E22. The conclusions of this study are that the developed integrated optimization strategy is effective in minimizing energy consumption while maintaining user comfort, and that the optimized control strategy outperforms the standard control in terms of energy efficiency. The study also concludes that the experimental in-situ optimization methodology for the water circulation pumps frequency is effective in achieving significant energy savings.. 
N1  - E23. The study concludes that the optimized control strategy is effective in improving energy performance and user comfort in both cooling and heating modes. The study also concludes that the design guideline provides a minimum volume for the buffer tank that is sufficient for the heat pump system.. 
N1  - E24. The conclusions of the study are that the energy optimization algorithm developed is effective in achieving energy savings while keeping user comfort, and that the algorithm can be used to optimize the performance of GSHP systems.. 
N1  - E25. The conclusions of the study highlight the effectiveness of the optimized control methodology in improving energy performance while maintaining user comfort, with potential applications to other ground source heat pump installations. 
